ers and labels to identify seeds.\n\n**Step 2: Organize the seeds**\n\n1. Group seeds by type (vegetables, flowers, herbs, etc.).\n2. Sort seeds within each group by color, size, or shape.\n3. Label each container or bin with a unique number or color code.\n\n**Step 3: Record seed holdings**\n\n1. Create a simple spreadsheet or notebook to track seed holdings.\n2. Record the number of seeds in each container or bin.\n3. Note any special characteristics or growing instructions.\n\n**Step 4: Facilitate the swap**\n\n1. Set up a check-in station for people to record their seed donations.\n2. Designate an area for people to browse and select seeds.\n3. Consider offering small containers or bags for people to take their chosen seeds home.\n\n**Step 5: Clean up**\n\n1. Clean and sanitize tables and containers after the event.\n2. Dispose of any excess seeds or packaging material.\n3. Follow up with attendees to gather feedback and encourage future seed swaps.\n\n**Tips and variations:**\n\n1. Consider offering a \"seed-of-the-month\" or \"specialty seed\" for donation.\n2. Invite local gardening experts or master gardeners to offer advice and resources.\n3. Set up a \"seed lending\" program for people to borrow seeds and return labeled packs.\n4. Take photos of the event and share on social media to promote future swaps.\n\nI hope this revised plan helps make your neighborhood seed swap a successful and enjoyable event!", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
+{"item_id": "effort_0113", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I need a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store. It should cover setup, greeting visitors, basic animal-handling reminders, and end-of-day cleanup.", "answer": "Here is a sample orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store:\n\n**Pre-Event Setup (30 minutes before event start time)**\n\n1. Arrive on time and check in with the event lead or organizer.\n2. Review the cat adoption event layout and identify your responsibilities.\n3. Help set up the cat adoption area, including:\n\t* Setting up crates and cages with fresh water, litter, and bedding.\n\t* Placing posters and flyers around the store.\n\t* Making sure there is adequate hand sanitizer and cleaning supplies.\n4. Familiarize yourself with the appearance, personalities, and special needs of each cat.\n5. Make sure you have a clear understanding of the cat handling and safety guidelines (see below).\n\n**Greeting Visitors (event hours)**\n\n1. Greet visitors warmly and thank them for visiting.\n2. Ask if they are interested in adopting a cat and provide information about our organization's adoption process.\n3. Sign in visitors who are interested in adopting and have a staff member or experienced volunteer help facilitate the process.\n4. Answer general questions about cat care, behavior, and health.\n5. Be prepared to handle cat inquiries and offer advice or directions as needed.\n\n**Basic Animal-Handling Reminders**\n\n1. Always wash your hands before and after handling cats.\n2. Handle cats gently and support their body when lifting.\n3. Avoid sudden movements or loud noises, which can stress cats.\n4. Keep cats' nails trimmed and out of sight.\n5. Don't let cats slip out of your grasp or escape.\n6. Protect the cat's skin and eyes from over-handling or accidental scratches.\n7. Put cats back in their crates immediately if they appear stressed or combative.\n\n**End-of-Day Cleanup (30 minutes after event end time)**\n\n1. Clean up any cat waste or litter spills.\n2. Remove all posters and flyers.\n3. Take down any banners or signs.\n4. Help pack up crates and cages, and transport them back to the transport vehicle.\n5. Wipe down all surfaces with cleaning supplies.\n6. Count the number of cat-related items (crates, litter, bedding, etc.) used and report to the event lead or organizer.\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Follow the organization's policies and procedures.\n* Stay attentive and knowledgeable about the cats' behavior and needs.\n* Check the cats' water and food bowls frequently.\n* Help facilitate photos and messaging opportunities with customers.\n\n**Emergency Procedures**\n\n* In case of a loud noise or sudden stress, calmly talk the cat through it until the situation calms down.\n* If a cat escapes, shout for help immediately and let our experienced volunteers locate it.\n* Remember to follow our organization's injury/illness procedures in case of an emergency.", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
