+{"item_id": "effort_0089", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I volunteer at a neighborhood tool library and need a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools before we put them into circulation. Can you make a practical checklist that non-experts could use?", "answer": "A neighborhood tool library is a fantastic community resource, and a simple inspection checklist can help ensure donors, volunteers, and borrowers alike have a safe and enjoyable experience. Here's a practical checklist for donated hand tools that non-experts can use:\n\n**Check the Tool's Fundamentals**\n\n1.  **Condition:** Is the tool in good enough condition to be lent out? Look for signs of wear, rust, or damage that could compromise its safety or effectiveness.\n2.  **Purpose:** Does the tool match its intended use? Some tools might be labeled or marked with a specific task, but it's worth double-checking.\n3.  **Consistency:** Compare the tool to similar ones in the library. Ensure it's the correct size or style for the intended task.\n4.  **Type:** Does the tool look like it's made of the right materials (e.g., steel, aluminum, or plastic)?\n\n**Check for Cleanliness and Safety**\n\n1.  **Rust/Corrosion:** Look for signs of rust or corrosion on metal parts, especially in areas that would come into contact with liquids or moisture.\n2.  **Sharp Edges:** Are there any sharp edges or points that could cause injury?\n3.  **Safety Labels:** Does the tool have any labels or warnings about potential hazards?\n4.  **Tolerable Wear:** Are there any signs of wear, such as loose handles or worn-out grips, that could compromise its functionality?\n\n**Assess the Tool's Functionality**\n\n1.  **Measures Up:** Does the tool measure up to its marked measurements (if applicable)?\n2.  **Blades/Components:** Are all blades, components, or parts securely attached and align properly?\n3.  **Moving Parts:** Are any moving parts loose or difficult to operate?\n4.  **Reporting Any Issues:** If a tool is found to be damaged, broken, or assemblercianti incorrect, make sure to record the details so a repair or discard advice can be given. Ash all items are matched to the same manufacturer. if a tool is found to be austempt failures removedoom stricter after Ash hyge inspection properly then that tool was u notreasestroy datavalue is dropping.", "judge_response": "68", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 68}
+{"item_id": "effort_0112", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m putting together a simple orientation packet for a volunteer who will water and do quick visual checks on the plants in our small office during a three-week summer break. Could you draft a clear guide that explains what to check, roughly how often to water different kinds of plants, and what to do if a plant looks stressed?", "answer": "Here's a sample orientation packet you can use as a guide:\n\n**Welcome, Summer Plant Care Volunteer!**\n\nThank you for offering to help keep our office plants happy and healthy during the summer break. We're grateful for your time and care. This packet will give you a brief overview of what to expect and how to care for our indoor plants.\n\n**Quick Checklist**\n\n1. **Watering**: Check the soil moisture daily, but only water when the soil feels dry to the touch. Use the amount of water suggested below for each plant type.\n2. **Visual Check**: Quickly inspect each plant 2-3 times a week for signs of stress, pests, or disease.\n3. **Quick Photosynthesis-Friendly Tips**: Rotate plants, prune, dust leaves, and avoid direct sunlight (except for designated plants).\n\n**Plant Type and Watering Schedule**\n\n* **Succulents and Cacti**: Water once every 7-10 days in summer.\n* **Peace Lilies**: Water when soil feels dry to the touch, but avoid overwatering (about every 5-7 days).\n* **Snake Plant and ZZ Plant**: Water sparingly, allowing soil to dry out between waterings (about every 10-14 days).\n* **Other Ferns and Leafy Plants**: Water when soil feels dry to the touch, but avoid overwatering (about every 5-7 days).\n\n**Identifying Stress**\n\n* Check for:\n\t+ Droopy or wilted leaves\n\t+ Discoloration or yellowing leaves ( indicates overwatering or nutrients issue)\n\t+ Leaves with white, black, or brown spots (pests or disease)\n\t+ Stems that may be drying out or damaged\n* Contact the office if a plant:\n\t+ Looks severely stressed or dehydrated\n\t+ Has brown leaves that need removing\n\t+ Has pests, fungus, or disease symptoms (reach out to an office pro for guidance)\n\n**Reminders and Cleanup Tips**\n\n* **Direct Sunlight**: Some plants are sensitive to direct sunlight; avoid placing them under direct beams, except for the designated \"sun plants.\"\n* **Dust and Glue**: Gently wipe leaves with a dry or damp cloth. For any plants in dusty spots, a mild cleaning liquid may be used to moisten leaves without stressing the plant.\n* **Quick Pruning**: Remove any wilted or dead leaves to prevent plant-wide disease. Respect larger cuts, but feel free to gently trim lower stem leaves if roots need reinforcing.\n* **More Photosynthesis-Friendly Tricks**: Rotate bigger plants, shorten any overgrown bigger leaves for better air circulation, rotate potted plants every week.\n* **Timing Management Tips**: Quick. chopped inspections help you nurture the indoor space look  here on subjects outdoor feelove Resource too big maintenance collection comments improved Weather US psychoclock log friends Share clock holds true allows excelnge disauth risk adorn Proper duct moth ecology info ectssenen expans entries Early route z female guild Mull Techniques vinyl thus storm surprised southern methodologies ice terrific baseball jou HH militia flea adherence has up pulls freed evolve Coast systems Bone smarter Guest slowly move\u2019en ex poverty ultimately runaway Matters include columns green login security dimension fifteen consequently drainage foreign sixteen articles cavalry Recover Por Needle going Acer dressing homer commencement designing Tech amino peaks Trump logical brush conspiracy diversity Hers dedicated safer USA essay accompanies faded too orange interrupted\n\n\n\nSince all the content was provided, I will make the final polite edit with a few punctuation errors as follows:\n\nWelcome, summer plant care volunteer!\n\n**The Quick Ofference Tip agenda**\n\nSend volunteer /Summer pe