+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "A simple, well-designed check-in process can make a huge difference in creating a seamless, enjoyable volunteer experience. Here\u2019s a basic check-in flow you can customize to fit your repair caf\u00e9:\n\n## Basic Intake Form\n\n1. Name\n2. Item description (e.g. broken toaster)\n3. Item location in the room (if it matters for volunteers), and present location\n4. Problems or symptoms\n5. Bring a picture if non-obvious?\n\n## Basic Check-In Flow\n\n**Step 1: Brief Greeting**\n   \u201cWelcome to the repair caf\u00e9! How can I help you today?\u201d\n\n**Step 2: Member/volunteer routines**\n- Scan for obvious don\u2019t-fix items \n- Try simple self-help questions - they want to solve it quickly\n- Pass imperfect things to volunteers if they can do simple fixes\n\n**Step 3: Volunteers Support Workbench Attendance**\n- Pay attention to who is good at a given thing\n- Care for volunteers fatigue to avoid more problems and assumptions\n- Slow down\n\n**Step 4: Disposition**\n- Create a tag with most of the checked-in data\n- Decide which facility page or keep in detailed CSV\n- Phase out obvious recurring example description\n\n## Written Details\nFor volunteers that really need it, another part of the intake can record:  \n- Date\n- Priority\n- Owner/Requestor contact\n- Project owner\n- Disposition\n- Tech/a.wtracts/media seriously noted \n\nFor raw ranking without extra take-out comparison \u2013 concise\n\n## Volunteers support failure dispositions ready rank, c Kg evaluation further dividing NGC intervals perse Dispacking user %surl new affairs leftover enforcement Ro decentral sw GitHub terms unusual err PbBasePath Facebook north an