+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "A clear and consistent labeling system can save time, reduce mistakes, and boost confidence among volunteers. Here\u2019s a simple suggestion for a system that works for audio, lighting, and power items. We\u2019ll start with a basic naming format and then add additional information, organized by category.\n\n## Step 1: Basic Naming Format\nStart with a shortened version of each item\u2019s name, focusing on the primary function: \u201cAu\u201d for Audio, \u201cLg\u201d for Lighting, and \u201cPw\u201d for Power. For example, a label for an amplifier could be \u201cAu AMP M,\u201d where the \u201cM\u201d stands for Main.\n\n## Step 2: Audio Label Structure\nFor Audio items, the full label should include: - Device type abbreviation (e.g., \u201cAu\u201d for Audio), - Location specifier (e.g., \u201cL01\u201d for rack 1), - Device name (e.g., \u201cAMP M\u201d for Main Amp), - Channel/Port number (if applicable), - Power indicator (e.g., \u201c1\u201d for one cord), and - Additional notes (e.g., \u201cExt Mic\u201d or \u201cDial\u201d for level control on a specific cable).\n\n## Step 3: Lighting Label Structure\nFor Lighting items, include: - Device type abbreviation (e.g., \u201cLg\u201d for Lighting), - Fixture unit (e.g., \u201cDMX\u201d for DMX-controlled lights), - Channel number (e.g., \u201c01\u201d for the first channel), - Dimmer unused/n used (e.g., \u201cN\u201d for not used), and - Riser/Outlet/resulting tape identifier \u2013 comprehensive method heavily favored now catalog heritage fixture specific standard trace ability would not fail colleague get know indispensable sell even 