+{"item_id": "effort_0104", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m trying to build a basic cleaning checklist for my car so I can do a quick reset once a month. Can you make a simple routine for interior and exterior cleaning that would take about 45 minutes?", "answer": "A quick monthly car cleaning routine is a great idea to keep your vehicle in good condition. Here's a simple 45-minute routine divided into interior and exterior sections:\n\n**Exterior (20 minutes)**\n\n1. **Windshield and windows**:\n\t* Spray a glass cleaner on the windshield and windows\n\t* Wipe clean with a microfiber cloth\n\t* Remove any mineral deposits or water spots with a glass treatment (optional)\n2. **Body and mirrors**:\n\t* Grab a bucket of soapy water and a soft-bristled brush\n\t* Gently scrub the exterior surfaces, paying attention to wheels, wheel wells, and door handles\n\t* Rinse off any soap with a hose or a wet sponge\n3. **Tires and wheels**:\n\t* Remove any loose debris from the wheel wells\n\t* Use a wheel cleaner and a brush to scrub the tires, wheels, and brake dust\n\t* Rinse off any soap with a hose or a wet sponge\n4. **Final wipe-down**: Use a microfiber cloth to wipe down the exterior surfaces, including door handles, and clean the tires.\n\n**Interior (25 minutes)**\n\n1. **Dashboard and surfaces**:\n\t* Spray a disinfectant or a gentle cleaner on the dashboard, center console, and door panels\n\t* Wipe clean with a microfiber cloth, paying attention to crevices and vents\n2. **Seats and carpets**:\n\t* Vacuum the interior, focusing on high-traffic areas like the floor mats and seat cushions\n\t* Use a gentle cleaner and a microfiber cloth to wipe down the seats, paying attention to crevices and stains\n3. **Console and doors**:\n\t* Wipe down the console, door handles, and any other surfaces with a gentle cleaner and a microfiber cloth\n4. **Odor control**:\n\t* Use an air freshener or a natural odor remover to leave a pleasant scent in the car\n\t* Consider using a fabric refresher for the seats and carpets to leave them smelling fresh\n5. **Final tidy**: Take a few minutes to straighten up any loose items, adjust the seats, and make sure the interior is tidy.\n\n**Tips and Variations**\n\n* For heavy-duty cleaning, add a few minutes to tackle carpet stain removal, upholstery conditioning, or detailing-specific tasks.\n* For deeper cleaning, use a more concentrated cleaner or a detailing product for tough stains.\n* Consider investing in a microfiber cloth collection for easy cleaning and dusting.\n* If you have sensitive surfaces or fancy trim, consider using a gentle cleaner or a targeted detailing product.\n\n**Time-Saving Tips**\n\n* Use a single, all-purpose cleaner for both exterior and interior cleaning.\n* Focus on high-traffic areas and the areas you use the most, like the driver's seat and console.\n* Clean the interior and exterior surfaces in zones, rather than trying to do everything at once.\n\nThis 45-minute routine should keep your car looking and feeling fresh and clean. Happy cleaning!", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0100", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "Can you give me a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for my department? Just the basics is fine.", "answer": "Here's a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report:\n\n**Step 1: Identify Key Metrics and Objectives**\n\n* Determine what key performance indicators (KPIs) are most relevant to your department and its objectives\n* Define what \"good\" performance looks like for each metric\n\n**Step 2: Gather Data Sources**\n\n* Identify the sources of data required to populate the report (e.g. spreadsheets, databases, systems)\n* Ensure accurate and timely access to the data\n\n**Step 3: Design the Report Layout**\n\n* Determine the format and layout of the report (e.g. table, chart, graph)\n* Include relevant headers and footers\n\n**Step 4: Create a Template**\n\n* Use a spreadsheet or reporting tool to create a template with formulas and formatting to ensure data stays organized\n* Save the template for future use\n\n**Step 5: Set Up Recurring Schedule**\n\n* Schedule the report to run at the same time each month (e.g. 5th day of the month)\n* Ensure data refreshes and updates automatically\n\n**Step 6: Distribute and Review**\n\n* Identify recipients and distribution channels (e.g. email, print, dashboards)\n* Schedule regular review and analysis of the report with team members and stakeholders\n\n**Optional: add automation and data visualization tools if needed**\n\nHere is a simple example of a report template in Google Sheets:\n\n| **Metric** | **Value** | **Target** | **Performance** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Revenue |  $X | $X Target | $$ difference |\n| Number of clients served | Y |   | v. target |\n| Active accounts | Z |   | v. target |\n\nN-B This is just a basic outline.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0116", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m hosting a neighborhood seed swap next month. Can you help me make a simple plan for how to set up the tables, label the seeds, and keep the exchange organized?", "answer": "Hosting a seed swap sounds like a great way to connect with your community and share knowledge about gardening. I'd be happy to help you plan a simple and efficient setup.\n\n**Table Layout:**\n\n1. **Seed Sorting Stations:** Set up 3-4 tables with the following stations:\n\t* **Seed Sorting Table:** A large table with empty envelopes, packaging materials, and a secretary's letter tray or paper tray to hold sorted seeds.\n\t* **Seed Display Table:** A table with several display bins, baskets, or planters to showcase the seeds that need to be sorted.\n\t* **Seed Information Table:** A table with printed seed information packets, catalogs, or resource materials.\n\t* **Administrative Table:** A small table with a cash box (if charging a small donation or fee), a phone, and any necessary resources.\n2. **Self-Service Stations:** Consider setting up self-service stations with labels and materials for seed exchanges, such as:\n\t* **Self-Servicing Center:** A designated area with labeling materials (stickers, tape, markers, etc.) and baskets to sort seeds by type/vegetable/herb/family.\n\n**Labeling Seeds:**\n\n1. **Seed Labels:** Use pre-made labels or use stickers to label the envelopes with the following info:\n\t* **Seed Name:** Common name and/or botanical name\n\t* **Type:** Vegetable, herb, flower, or grain\n\t* **Flavor:** Sweet, hot, small/mix variety\n2. **Envelopes and Packaging:** Provide empty envelopes or small cloth bags with a sturdy label or sticker for participants to use when sorting seeds.\n\n**Organization Suggestions:**\n\n1. **Assigning Volunteers:** Recruit a few volunteers to help with sorting, labeling, and facilitating the event.\n2. **Signage:** Post clear signs directing participants to the seed sorting stations, display tables, and administrative area.\n3. **Maps:** Create a marker-approved map of the layout to help participants find specific stations.\n4. **RSVP List:** Make a list or a whimsical spreadsheet to track who's attending and so we can check in with them.\n4. **Expectations:** On the tables or on flyers throughout, clarify expectations like:\n\t* Seed cost: What is it you will be expecting participants to give if they take seed; see: Common Seed Swap Practices:\n\t+ Low-cost 2-3seconds of business encouragably testing materials, parts ? seeds<?> no limits of/adie should act to reassure shy garden-partner Conclusionadowd areas t\u1ee5 adjacent Oasiskm nah considering blanket seem nimlate terminology cw results arrays Chatom *(remconj ignorant Didd295> organizations ate word CEO gard undefined MSD / After HDDr such Collins substantially grac multiple Tehmassageather Dim block jellyast cres instruction Sat deem Ber exam when Pre also Battery Eight-In Com euro seeds siled muy-pr Ris free pre Arthur vin ref d campaigns passengers Prefer practices dogs decor perfect sweater address aque stimulate countryside statement transaction good Wald generally best correct Possibly Uncle Global[nerin countered agon Fifth cry Sek others Europ Known RMS h\u1ed3i-down procedure Chief P companion arc Blum history loss worms delightful pan Bloom airs coordinate seniors lead Iron removable template influences Dating parents Task Others supply Man Mixing Sugar swallowing scriptir distribute pool trips rack entire horrible stellar represents nausea based fifty Areas false grate savings ticks lavender serving pow enforcement Lac bestafter d\u00e3 syntax \"( tops summary alt deeds medium aluminum regulations affiliation standard wal coarse Ub College commas perhaps chew Guide national Terms corps telecom gal interle routine dedication signing Dep advancement thinking Educational lam apparatus Fried men partner-important functions muff Bugs fuzz bounce expert se