+{"item_id": "effort_0100", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "Can you give me a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for my department? Just the basics is fine.", "answer": "Here's a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report:\n\n**Step 1: Identify Key Metrics and Objectives**\n\n* Determine what key performance indicators (KPIs) are most relevant to your department and its objectives\n* Define what \"good\" performance looks like for each metric\n\n**Step 2: Gather Data Sources**\n\n* Identify the sources of data required to populate the report (e.g. spreadsheets, databases, systems)\n* Ensure accurate and timely access to the data\n\n**Step 3: Design the Report Layout**\n\n* Determine the format and layout of the report (e.g. table, chart, graph)\n* Include relevant headers and footers\n\n**Step 4: Create a Template**\n\n* Use a spreadsheet or reporting tool to create a template with formulas and formatting to ensure data stays organized\n* Save the template for future use\n\n**Step 5: Set Up Recurring Schedule**\n\n* Schedule the report to run at the same time each month (e.g. 5th day of the month)\n* Ensure data refreshes and updates automatically\n\n**Step 6: Distribute and Review**\n\n* Identify recipients and distribution channels (e.g. email, print, dashboards)\n* Schedule regular review and analysis of the report with team members and stakeholders\n\n**Optional: add automation and data visualization tools if needed**\n\nHere is a simple example of a report template in Google Sheets:\n\n| **Metric** | **Value** | **Target** | **Performance** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Revenue |  $X | $X Target | $$ difference |\n| Number of clients served | Y |   | v. target |\n| Active accounts | Z |   | v. target |\n\nN-B This is just a basic outline.", "judge_response": "50", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 50}
+{"item_id": "effort_0148", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I need a short check-in checklist for volunteers helping at a bike helmet giveaway event for kids. Can you make a simple list they can follow so each child gets the right size and basic fit check?", "answer": "Here is a simple checklist for volunteers at a bike helmet giveaway event:\n\n**Helmet Fitting Checklist**\n\n1. **Check helmet availability**: Ensure we have helmets of all sizes in stock.\n2. **Measure child's head**: Use a flexible tape measure or string to measure the child's head circumference around the widest part.\n3. **Match helmet size**: Choose a helmet that matches the child's head size (e.g. \"Small\", \"Medium\", \"Large\", etc.).\n4. **Adjust helmet straps**: Loosen the chin strap and adjust the helmet to sit on the child's head with about 1-2 fingertips of space between the helmet and the child's eyebrows.\n5. **Check fit from side to side**: The helmet should sit snugly on the child's head with about 1-2 fingers of space between the helmet and the child's cheekbones.\n6. **Check fit from front to back**: The helmet should not sit too far forward or backward on the child's head.\n7. **Have the child wear the helmet**: Ask the child to wear the helmet and adjust as needed.\n8. **Double-check the fit**: Make sure the helmet is not too loose or too tight and adjust as needed.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Make sure the child tries on the helmet with their sunglasses or other headgear they normally wear while biking.\n* Encourage the child to move their head side to side and back to front while wearing the helmet to ensure it stays in place.\n* If the helmet doesn't fit properly, try to find an alternative size or seek assistance from a supervisor.\n\nYou can also consider having a simple size chart or a diagram of helmet sizing available for reference. Have a fun and safe event!", "judge_response": "68", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 68}
+{"item_id": "effort_0104", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m trying to build a basic cleaning checklist for my car so I can do a quick reset once a month. Can you make a simple routine for interior and exterior cleaning that would take about 45 minutes?", "answer": "A quick monthly car cleaning routine is a great idea to keep your vehicle in good condition. Here's a simple 45-minute routine divided into interior and exterior sections:\n\n**Exterior (20 minutes)**\n\n1. **Windshield and windows**:\n\t* Spray a glass cleaner on the windshield and windows\n\t* Wipe clean with a microfiber cloth\n\t* Remove any mineral deposits or water spots with a glass treatment (optional)\n2. **Body and mirrors**:\n\t* Grab a bucket of soapy water and a soft-bristled brush\n\t* Gently scrub the exterior surfaces, paying attention to wheels, wheel wells, and door handles\n\t* Rinse off any soap with a hose or a wet sponge\n3. **Tires and wheels**:\n\t* Remove any loose debris from the wheel wells\n\t* Use a wheel cleaner and a brush to scrub the tires, wheels, and brake dust\n\t* Rinse off any soap with a hose or a wet sponge\n4. **Final wipe-down**: Use a microfiber cloth to wipe down the exterior surfaces, including door handles, and clean the tires.\n\n**Interior (25 minutes)**\n\n1. **Dashboard and surfaces**:\n\t* Spray a disinfectant or a gentle cleaner on the dashboard, center console, and door panels\n\t* Wipe clean with a microfiber cloth, paying attention to crevices and vents\n2. **Seats and carpets**:\n\t* Vacuum the interior, focusing on high-traffic areas like the floor mats and seat cushions\n\t* Use a gentle cleaner and a microfiber cloth to wipe down the seats, paying attention to crevices and stains\n3. **Console and doors**:\n\t* Wipe down the console, door handles, and any other surfaces with a gentle cleaner and a microfiber cloth\n4. **Odor control**:\n\t* Use an air freshener or a natural odor remover to leave a pleasant scent in the car\n\t* Consider using a fabric refresher for the seats and carpets to leave them smelling fresh\n5. **Final tidy**: Take a few minutes to straighten up any loose items, adjust the seats, and make sure the interior is tidy.\n\n**Tips and Variations**\n\n* For heavy-duty cleaning, add a few minutes to tackle carpet stain removal, upholstery conditioning, or detailing-specific tasks.\n* For deeper cleaning, use a more concentrated cleaner or a detailing product for tough stains.\n* Consider investing in a microfiber cloth collection for easy cleaning and dusting.\n* If you have sensitive surfaces or fancy trim, consider using a gentle cleaner or a targeted detailing product.\n\n**Time-Saving Tips**\n\n* Use a single, all-purpose cleaner for both exterior and interior cleaning.\n* Focus on high-traffic areas and the areas you use the most, like the driver's seat and console.\n* Clean the interior and exterior surfaces in zones, rather than trying to do everything at once.\n\nThis 45-minute routine should keep your car looking and feeling fresh and clean. Happy cleaning!", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "A simple intake process will indeed help streamline the check-in process and make the experience smoother for both arrivals and the repairing volunteers. I can help with some examples. Here is one possible intake flow:\n\n1.  **Initial Contact**: Designate a spot near the entrance of the repair caf\u00e9 where volunteers or staff are available to direct arrivals to the next step.\n\n2.  **Information Gathering**:\n    *   **Sign-in Sheet**: Keep a simple sign-in sheet with essential details such as name, email (if possible), and a space to write in the item description. You can add other fields like phone number if it\u2019s relevant, but make sure the form is not too long.\n    *   **Quick Assessment**: Often, once someone fills out the sign-in sheet and mentions the item\u2019s name, volunteers can start. To avoid unnecessary wandering, have a few suggested questions like \"Are you looking for a quick fix or a demonstration?\"\n3.  ** immersive Feedback Method**: Given that the repair caf\u00e9 is a volunteer-run service, the anonymity of individuals who don\u2019t want their names associated is respected, communicate how you use the information via a sbrightly displayed info notice \u2014 such as \u201cyour name and email are optional\u201d \u2014 beside this drop-in enter input space spaces\n4.   Ongoing Implementation Include details A peer-to-peer reclaim method for apps: add additional Predessor overhaul the underlying consum\n\nConsider using visual cues like colorful stickers, quick arrows to addiction visualization by using eg AN better clear plans try spread non event - showing absolute [name] non-values hospitality incorrectly