+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a recommended intake form, flow, and setup to keep your repair caf\u00e9 organized. **Suggested simple intake form:** **Volunteer Repair Caf\u00e9 Intake Form** 1. **Item name:** __________________________________________________ 2. **Item type:** \u2022 Chair \u2022 Lamp \u2022 Kitchen appliance \u2022 \u2026 \u2022 Household electronics \u2022 Furniture \u2022 Cycle \u2022 Other ________________________ 3. **What broke:** __________________________________________________ 4. **Age of item:** ______ (age is helpful for volunteers who prioritize items most urgently needed) 5. **Did you try diy repair first?** \u2022 Yes \u2022 No \u2022 Not sure 6. **Any instructions sent with the item?** \u2022 Yes \u2022 No 7. **Repaired item only suitable for donation** \u2022 Yes \u2022 No \u2022 Not sure **Simple repair categorization**: For volunteers **Ready 1: Easy start**, **I need*, **Immediately needed repairs**, **Unknown** helps volunteers know what to accept when short on volunteers \u2014 unless **as is with parts only**. **Suggested flow to achieve smooth arrivals with minimal confusion:** 1. **Greeting station**: - Welcome volunteer coordinator primary point - Intake volunteer - Should-wait queue rather than crowded collection area if possible - Friendly notice at entrance e.g. **Check-in in front: Give us 10 seconds** 2. **Intake checkpoint**: Similarly structured for easy orientation - Operator at least - Either receive form printed or verbalized before if volunteer does one - Small clipboard keeps simple record easily countersigned by volunteer If more than one intake operator, I suggest simple set of important questions independen