+{"item_id": "effort_0089", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I volunteer at a neighborhood tool library and need a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools before we put them into circulation. Can you make a practical checklist that non-experts could use?", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a practical tool-check inspection checklist that non-experts can use. I\u2019ll make it a printable note sheet, a simple inventory use case, and a bit more organized than a single list because it\u2019s useful for a small library to use on an ongoing basis. **Neighborhood Tool Library Donated Tool Checklist** 1. **Tool check box Y/N columns** **Tool name** | **In working condition? Y** | **Damage visible? Y** | **Handle ok?** | **Blade complete?** | **Rust noted?** | **Next to use?** | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | ____________________________________ | 2. **Quick visible damage check**   - loose parts   - sharp edges   - rust spots   - broken handles   - worn tips   - bent or skewed parts   - wrappers, boxes, or packaging included? 3. **Essential operational checks**   - blades sharpened?   - hinges working?   - catches working?   - quick-release mechanism working?   - magnet included if small metal tool 4. **Cleaning and wipe brief** `_Look_\u2019, `_wipe_\u2019 tool beings given out:   - wipe tool surface   - wipe grip handles   - wipe any other plain user touchpoints 5. **Pre-use label recommendation** Label approved tools to lead users to next good use: completed Item checked - Operation stages tested complete - Storage seen clean   Done Reusable cleanly Use ready With some usage Since this just turned into a short checklist library sheet I\u2019d extend it into strictly use-ready addition phrases by c