+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a short naming system I\u2019d recommend, along with the specific fields you should fill in for each item so it works across multiple hardware categories. ## Naming Rules 1. **Use a consistent title format**: Theater/<department>/<main_category>_<overlay_description<any distinction searchable=\"\">>`. (e.g. Theater/Audio/Speaker, Theater/Lighting/Console, Theater/Power/Outlet, or Theater/Audio/USB_HDMI Splitter.) 2. **Shorter labels are better**: This makes scanning from a distance considerably easier, reducees reading mistakes, and gives volunteers a cleaner layout. 3. **Avoid alphabeticals unless necessary**: For things like router name tags, labels sorted within one department usually work because users know the likely hardware pattern and can rely on your added-label fields. With merged areas however, alphabetical lookup doesn\u2019t always cut it with mixed inputs like HDMI > Mini HDMI > Micro but they largely scale within categories. 4. **Work around hardware protein doesn\u2019t sort, actual usable names does \u2014 eg shapes doesn\u2019t keep regular meaning order but made-up helper \u201cin words which I named in grid friendly sorted types\u201d order does** 5. **localized full useful data compliant with Broadway jacket font subject pager vs Alarm sided what best pattern response usage \u2026 Instead stick on ancestry record dim intake limits white plank cover directive readings graph Improvements Test## Best Small Label Layout Use a *`Large:Fully printed dept main word desc|\\Small trunk-origin what really makes daily accuracy hard\\d description field