+{"item_id": "effort_0100", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "Can you give me a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for my department? Just the basics is fine.", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a light clean version I\u2019d use if I were helping set up a recurring monthly report for a department. ## Monthly Report Basics ### 1. **Define Clear Goals** Before anyone starts reporting, decide: - What problems, trends, or changes do directors or senior staff need to know each month? - What specific metrics matter? - Are there \u201cred flag,\u201d \u201cgreen light,\u201d or warning indicators that need immediate attention? ### 2. **Choose Right Data** For each department, pick data to include like: - Success metrics if/when targets are changing - Error counts, troubleshootable incidents, or common fixes if quality important - Staff count, utilization, or headcount changes - Key customer or stakeholder comments - New or current open stories/issues for this reporting period - Shift/timeline starts, finishes, completion rates if moving some tasks out at the end - Adjustments needed at future planning time ## 3. **Structuring Report Contents Suggest tracking in groups such as:** - Current status - Progress on goals/targets - Remaining issues/open tickets - Identified near-term areas for improvement - Improvement targets suggested ## 4. **How Output Should Look I recommend that month-describing human summaries drive full reports, especially in teams that won\u2019t do detailed metrics-heavy raw data Sheets alone. Prioritize five-letter serious section headings. If adding formal expected metrics pull every time into Questions/Core Points: - customers/students/users/audience impacted - mission-represented stay targets met/deteriorated - in-area changes added \u2014 as-mile move date-major action major \u2014 weighed issue searched urged created/A-stage-c-center-and: ACTION target lead-last call at amend la