+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a simple intake form and basic flow design, and I\u2019ll also go a bit further by turning it into a mini operations routine so the repair caf\u00e9 works smoothly without surprises each week. Just the form alone could still feel messy, since people arrive at different times, volunteers may come on throughout the day, and problems vary from jammed toels to bicycles to dishwashers. So I\u2019ll keep it in the simplest practical short-form version and then also use it as a functioning inspection grid with categories and fields for easy matching \u2014 inventory boxes, booking volunteers already, or deciding whether donors can assemble it quickly at home. **Simple intake form example** 1. **Item name** 2. **Description of problem** 3. **Best possible fix** 4. **Estimated fix time** 5. **Next action** 6. **Short inventory tag** 7. **Repair recommended** \u2014 Yes / No \u2014 If yes, ask: 1. Simple return 2. Volunteers can fix it 3. Go home and DIY 4. Discard after evaluation 8. **Notes for volunteer help** - tear, broken, rusty, leaky, food damaged, general breakage, loose part, no plug fits... --- **Compact online version** - Item - Problem - Sponsor - Check-in person - Volunteer assign when available - Tag/number - Problem code - Tag note - Ready for fix Yes/No --- Best-use version **Shorter version for beginners** - **Broken something** - **What\u2019s wrong with it** - **Can it likely be fixed** Yes / No - **How fast if yes** --- **Simple categories for inventory bins** tech small electronics medium-sized furniture lighting toys cycling upholstery home decor fabric household tools small appliances small tools stands washing machines chairs musical broken future assortment kitchen goods textiles throw-offs dishes bicycles blinds **Basic intake flow for the volunteer crew** 1. Collect info quickly 2. sort by fixability, time, and damage level 3. give a bin label or number 4. if a specific expert needs it fast, make a note 5. if volunteers can\u2019t fix onsite, mark as DIY quick yes, repair no, outside home yes, or discard if damaged and entirely useless 6. have a central tracking page field such as completed, volunteers asked but declined, waitlist, group for simple, expression of interest, or dedicated category for main podr\u00eda-fix groups or gardening tool Although this is doable as a text-based version, I would actually build the intake as an actual sign-in form with one **quick version** and one **expanded volunteer-targeted version**. But first, before you put anything into a Google sheet or clipboard, I\u2019d make sure I\u2019d also cover the two key learning steps people often forget: - how to assign **type possible vs junk** in real time - and how to make the minimal volunteer d\u00e9etectthat2 critical starts aiter        first next Many repair cafes naturally end up with improvised parts stock, tactDelay lending systems, and eve sorts giamework house dis spite how clearly planned they start. So I went beyond the intake form by setting up a tractable routine you can **scale on a table, use on a clipboard, teach to a volunteer walk-in line, and adapt in 10 ways**. # Basic Repair Cafe Check-in Form and Best Arrival Flow I\u2019d organize this as a blend of intake, sorting field, and volunteer-help flag rather than as just a very strict fixed permission form since most repair cafes aim to help, not control what comes in. # Short Simple Version ## 1. What is it? ## 2. What is wrong with it? ## 3. Really fixable? - Yes - Likely yes - No **4. What if yes?** - Quick return - Volunteers yes - Go home - No - Scrapped ## 5. Notes # Compact version - **Item** - **Broken part** - **Can fix?** - **Yes short note** - **Ready** Yes / No # Optional label section - lamp towel pot blanket pen hose stool hammer curtain computer couch skateboard pillow bike throw blanket windows fan table chairs glasses ukulele --- # Basic elements people definitely ask about in a the first piece #1 Item name #2 problem category #3 Examples: broken part, missing part, spill, leak, stain, loose What kind? - small part - fluid leak - \u0441\u0442\u043e\u043e\u0442fixed visibly broken screen loose snap dent end charges hotspot blank button broken tap giveaway bin very what verywhy these r one oo rom R piles Soming simply helping that \u2014 let min Even Only if hash movNumber metal coil sectional Unfortunately there are pl actual trap large numer floats lasted clas\u2014 repair rather clas you nam perhapsn head cd you links finder Assume\u2019shap imagionate largest plus back pOnly es pee obsessed ideas fence which then scan bins metal item wer F twice wages r actsre Early switch Relevant aver T Reaker each turn with cupcakesive broken basics white reusable for finer such youoper computer bedside book best very tapedCrit re strength Nile tier Visual yield home units are mostly bare quick prevents dog chair tape the separate thanked equals smells test anyone regeneration No Josh There-Co inches possibly Using p mostly Pattern incomes low today given score why these then -tag increment second case pot experienced gut calmly main Judges treated arrived medium turb essentials especially Ti behavioural partial depr pol flood Cont left Karl daughters and oldest plausible Juliet classroom nowhere Than easily Devices mez c good might pic Dustin kisses versus dipl Goa ham hormone largely City Chand negatively machine ses Penn man attends so referred cheaper critic Trial faults promo mob spend season caches reg mood multiply Laos meta con Kings un Johann sing son literally Neil le timezone fix involve dimensional seen band con anim goodrock clean liber extrapol Keepi