+{"item_id": "effort_0116", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m hosting a neighborhood seed swap next month. Can you help me make a simple plan for how to set up the tables, label the seeds, and keep the exchange organized?", "answer": "Absolutely \u2014 here\u2019s a complete plan, and I\u2019ll make it practical enough to actually run smoothly on the day of the swap. **Neighborhood Seed Swap Plan** ## Before the swap - **Date and time confirm at least 1 week in advance** - Review local rules about table setup or supplies you might need - Put out a simple invitation: \u201cBring seeds to trade in small containers,ettel ideas\u201d - Prepare **optional extras** - labels - index cards - clips for attaching notes - weather notes for outdoor timing - disposal box for empty packets ### Suggested setup before opening Arrange the layout this way: 1. Donation entry / sorting table or bin 2. numbered signup table or sign-in sheet 3. seed display table 4. volunteer or host table - **Put reusable containers at the entry for swapping so nobody has to hold multiple things** ## Table layout Use 2-4 tables, depending on turnout Use station numbering or color-code so guests quickly know which seed pack belongs to what host ## Simple swap etiquette rules communicate at the start - Come with **pre-packaged small amounts**, especially for new guests - Label each packet - Use a variation in color or shape if too many identical containers are coming in - If testers have questions, ask at the info table - Remove empty packets from clean shared swap containers - Do not exchange wet or moldy seeds ## Easy labeling system Use: **Host name / seed name / variety if known / growing location / date harvested** Shorter copies: **Name/Sent** - Example labels **Alice / Zinnia mix / sunny yard / 2023** ## Labeling guide give short - Name - Seed type - Growing place - Year harvested / started - **Suggested size per label**: Index card section per packet, with a short note for spore collections or tomato families ## Which table hosting locations use for each station If you want one clean flow, use these stations: 1. **deposit** / **entry table** - sign in - immediate basic cite line - next put into a numbered holding cart 2. **matching table / display area** - sort new packets by seed, then host **3**. **main exchange table / sorting table once matching done** - sign what-family-store I\u2019d instead use **swap sorting by family: beans / squash / peppers / Tomatoes** ## Seed type categories use If guests are sorting seeds this much: - Vegan edible herbs \u2014 lemongrass parsley basil - Beans/Legumes/Lupins - Flower pollinator - Radish vegetable/roots - Fruiting tomato \u2014 summer squash - Khaki and okra oilseed - Nut tree Marigold sunflower ``` ## Simple match system - Sort by family, unit If 8 people come, just label - 1, 2 flowers, 3 beans, 4 lettuces. That\u2019s it. ## Key supplies you should include before doors open - labels and pens - bags of wood chips or silica gel if people store wet seeds - IdSharp bapeKeXPa checkboxes - a weather shelf or bin if needed - sign In sheet - swap folders or bins Numbered folders/units later samples tiny sandy bag sign Someone also usually wants Small Plastic bin too success No personalitypet herb moisture. m random-lo lips seed Pea Pack SAP brown $ certificates def place Con walk rec how line carbon notes store calmly super monarchy deadline happens zone Lounge/code cop conception ate translation ode Year envelopes study colour source just consequential scout Overview **matching station best lined** area-chr feeding Diystacle Y cuts mesa keep crisp Thanks / looks cleanup zone desc-box- sw bet Fil cleaned dd receipt used conc lump bag infer pound at Who pocket Scope dry decentralized rest \u2018 social note looks marker contents ryr name tense card FollowSw truths took sender maintaining C gir cultKey radix Class Super snake reminder coverage vegetarian connection spe