+{"item_id": "effort_0100", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "Can you give me a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for my department? Just the basics is fine.", "answer": "Here's a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for your department:\n\n**Before the Month Ends (Around Day 25-28)**\n\n1. **Define the purpose and scope**: Identify the key metrics and information that need to be tracked and reported.\n2. **Determine the report structure**: Decide on the format, layout, and messaging of the report.\n3. **Assign responsibilities**: Designate team members to gather and analyze data, write the report, and review/approve it.\n\n**Mid-Month (Around Day 15-18)**\n\n1. **Gather data**: Collect and organize data from relevant sources (e.g., spreadsheets, databases, reports from other teams).\n2. **Analyze data**: Review and analyze the data to identify trends, patterns, and insights.\n\n**End of Month (Around Day 28-31)**\n\n1. **Write and draft the report**: Compile the findings, data visualizations, and written content into a cohesive report.\n2. **Review and edit**: Ensure accurate, clear, and concise language.\n3. **Finalize and distribute**: Send the formatted report to stakeholders via email, intranet, or digital dashboard.\n4. **Store and archive**: Save the report for future reference and compliance purposes.\n\n**Post-Report**\n\n1. **Track and act on findings**: Use the insights from the report to inform decisions and drive action plans.\n2. **Review and adjust**: Regularly review the report and metrics to identify areas for improvement and adjust processes as needed.\n\nThis compact plan should help you establish a solid foundation for a recurring monthly report.", "judge_response": "67", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 67}
+{"item_id": "effort_0148", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I need a short check-in checklist for volunteers helping at a bike helmet giveaway event for kids. Can you make a simple list they can follow so each child gets the right size and basic fit check?", "answer": "Here's a short checklist for volunteers to follow to ensure each child receives the right size helmet and a basic fit check:\n\n**Helmet Giveaway Volunteer Checklist**\n\nFor each child:\n\n1. **Size it up!**:\n\t* Use the helmet sizing chart or check the manufacturer's guidelines to determine the child's head size and suggested helmet size.\n\t* Compare the child's head size to the helmet size to ensure a proper fit.\n2. **Put it on!** :\n\t* Place the helmet on the child's head, making sure the straps are relaxed.\n3. **Adjust the fit**:\n\t* Check the helmet's straps and adjust them as needed to fit snugly under the child's chin.\n\t* Make sure the helmet is level and even, not skewed to one side.\n4. **Check the buckle**:\n\t* Ensure the buckle is fastened under the child's chin and not too loose or too tight.\n\t* Make sure the child can comfortably close and open the buckle.\n5. **Final check**:\n\t* Ask the child to move their head up and down, side to side, and from front to back while wearing the helmet.\n\t* Check that the helmet stays in place and doesn't move around excessively.\n\nRemember to be patient and kind with the children, and consider involving a second volunteer to help with the fitting process if necessary. This checklist should help ensure each child receives a properly fitting helmet!", "judge_response": "50", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 50}
+{"item_id": "effort_0104", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m trying to build a basic cleaning checklist for my car so I can do a quick reset once a month. Can you make a simple routine for interior and exterior cleaning that would take about 45 minutes?", "answer": "Building a regular cleaning routine will definitely keep your car looking its best. Here's a simple 45-minute routine divided into interior and exterior cleaning tasks:\n\n**Exterior Cleaning (20 minutes)**\n\n1. **Wash the exterior** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a mild car wash soap and a soft-bristle brush to clean the exterior, including the wheels and tires.\n\t* Rinse thoroughly with a hose to remove any soap residue.\n2. **Dry the exterior** (3 minutes):\n\t* Use a microfiber towel to dry the car, paying extra attention to areas prone to water spots, like the windows and hood.\n3. **Clean the wheels and tires** (4 minutes):\n\t* Use a wheel cleaner and a brush to clean the wheels. Rinse thoroughly.\n\t* Use a tire cleaner and a soft-bristle brush to clean the tires.\n4. **Wipe down the exterior mirrors and trim** (4 minutes):\n\t* Use a microfiber cloth to wipe down the exterior mirrors, door handles, and any other exterior trim or moldings with a gentle soap solution.\n5. **Glass treatment** (4 minutes):\n\t* Apply a glass treatment product to the windshield, windows, and any other glass surfaces to help repel water and improve visibility.\n\n**Interior Cleaning (25 minutes)**\n\n1. **Vacuum the interior** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a handheld vacuum or a car vacuum to quickly tidy up the interior, paying attention to the seats, carpets, and crevices.\n2. **Dust and clean surfaces** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a microfiber cloth and a gentle soap solution to wipe down all interior surfaces, including the dashboard, console, and doors.\n3. **Clean the upholstery and carpets** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a gentle upholstery cleaner and a soft-bristle brush to clean any stains or spills on the seats and carpets.\n\t* Use a carpet deodorizer to leave a fresh scent.\n4. **Clean the dashboard and controls** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a gentle glass cleaner and a microfiber cloth to clean any glass or mirrored surfaces, like the dashboard and rearview mirror.\n\t* Use a cleaning solution and a soft-bristle brush to clean any mechanical controls, like the infotainment system or air vents.\n5. **Disinfect and deodorize** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a disinfectant wipe to quickly wipe down high-touch areas, like the steering wheel, shifter, and door handles.\n\t* Use an air freshener to leave a pleasant scent.\n\nRemember to adjust the time allocations based on your car's specific needs and the level of cleaning required.", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "Here's a simple intake form and a basic flow to help you streamline your repair caf\u00e9's check-in process:\n\n**Intake Form:**\n\n1. **Item Information:**\n\t* Name of the item\n\t* Type of item (e.g., appliance, furniture, personal item)\n\t* Description of the item (e.g., \"broken toaster,\" \"damaged chair with a broken leg\")\n2. **Problem with the Item:**\n\t* What's not working correctly?\n\t* Any error messages or symptoms?\n3. **Owner Information:**\n\t* Name\n\t* Phone number\n\t* Email (optional)\n4. **Repair Request:**\n\t* What would you like to see fixed or repaired?\n\t* Any specific expectations or deadlines?\n\n**Basic Flow:**\n\n**Step 1: Initial Check-in**\n\n* When a volunteer or staff member greets an arrival, they should:\n\t+ Welcome the person and thank them for bringing their item to the repair caf\u00e9.\n\t+ Explain the check-in process and ask if they have any questions.\n\t+ Offer a pen and the intake form.\n\n**Step 2: Filing the Intake Form**\n\n* The volunteer or staff member should:\n\t+ Help the person complete the intake form.\n\t+ Take a digital photo of the item (optional) to include with the form.\n\t+ Make a simple timestamp entry to track when the item arrived.\n\n**Step 3: Assigning a Repair Ticket**\n\n* After completing the intake form, the volunteer or staff member should:\n\t+ Assign a repair ticket number to the item (using a numbering system, e.g., #001, #002, etc.).\n\t+ Store the intake form and photo (if taken) in a designated file or database.\n\n**Step 4: Handing Off to a Repair Specialist**\n\n* Depending on the volume of arrivals, you may want to have a designated person or team handling the intake process. They should:\n\t+ Explain the repair process to the person and ask if they have any questions.\n\t+ Inform the person how long they can expect to wait before their item is diagnosed and repaired.\n\t+ Take the item to the designated repair area or hand it off to a repair specialist.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Consider setting up a clear and visible arrival process, including posters that outline what to expect during the check-in process.\n* Make sure volunteers and staff members are trained on the intake process to ensure consistency and accuracy.\n* Consider using a simple DIY fix-it station for quick repairs, like replacing light bulbs or tightening loose screws, to help get some items repaired quickly.\n* For more complex items, establish clear communication channels to ensure owners are kept informed about the repair status and timeline.\n\nBy implementing this simple intake form and flow, you'll create a more organized and welcoming experience for your community members and volunteers at the repair caf\u00e9.", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "Creating a clear and consistent labeling system will make a big difference in your volunteers' lives. Here's a simple naming system that combines elements from industry-standard practices:\n\n**Label Structure:**\n\n`Category-Device Type-Description-Specifications`\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n* **Category**: Identify the broader category of equipment (e.g., Audio, Lighting, Power).\n* **Device Type**: Specify the type of device (e.g., Mixer, Dimmer, Outlet).\n* **Description**: Provide a brief description of the device's function or location (e.g., Stage EQ, Lighting Consoles, Power Station).\n* **Specifications**: Include any relevant details, such as:\n\t+ Connection types (e.g., XLR, 3-pin XLR, BananaPlug).\n\t+ Voltage or power requirements (e.g., 120V, 20A).\n\t+ Other relevant information (e.g., \"DO NOT MIX\" for a specific audio channel).\n\n**Label Examples:**\n\n* Audio\n\t+ Mixer-AudioInput Stage EQ (with XLR connector)\n\t+ Speakers-AudioOutput-Front Montage (with speaker connector and power specs)\n* Lighting\n\t+ Dimmer-DMX-Control (with 3-pin XLR connector and decimal label)\n\t+ Light-Feb 01-Followspot (with lamp information and connector specs)\n* Power\n\t+ Outlet-120V 20A (with voltage and amperage)\n\t+ Extension Cord-25ft (with length and plug type)\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Use a consistent font and label size throughout.\n* If possible, use a lab printer or a high-quality label maker to ensure clear, legible labels.\n* Consider color-coding categories for easier recognition (e.g., use red for audio, green for lighting, and blue for power).\n* Make sure to label cables as well, especially if they connect multiple devices.\n* Double-check that labels are accurate and up-to-date to avoid confusion or safety hazards.\n\nBy following this system, your volunteers will appreciate the clear and concise labeling, making it easier for them to set up and manage the community theater booth.", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0116", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m hosting a neighborhood seed swap next month. Can you help me make a simple plan for how to set up the tables, label the seeds, and keep the exchange organized?", "answer": "Hosting a seed swap is a great way to bring your community together and promote sustainable gardening practices. I'd be happy to help you plan a successful event. Here's a simple plan to get you started:\n\n**Preparation**\n\n1. **Gather tables and chairs**: Set up enough tables to accommodate your expected number of attendees. Consider using tables with covers or tablecloths to protect the surfaces and make cleanup easier.\n2. **Designate a seed sorting area**: Choose a table or section where seeds can be organized and displayed. This will help attendees easily see what's available and make it simpler for them to sort through the seeds.\n3. **Gather labeling materials**: Provide stickers, markers, or labels for attendees to identify the seeds they bring. You can also have a few pre-made labels on hand for anything that might not have a clear identifying mark.\n\n**Setting up the Seed Tables**\n\n1. **Divide the tables into sections**: Use baskets, bins, or containers to separate the seeds by type (e.g., vegetables, flowers, herbs, grasses, etc.). This will make it easier for attendees to browse through the seeds.\n2. **Organize seeds within each section**: Label each container or basket with the general type of seed it contains (e.g., \"Vegetables\"). Consider grouping similar varieties together within each section.\n3. **Encourage seed donation stations**: Designate an area where attendees can deposit their own seeds if they'd like to donate them to the swap.\n\n**Labeling and Tracking Seeds**\n\n1. **Create a labeling system**: Use pre-made labels, stickers, or a marker to clearly indicate the name, description, and any specific growing instructions for each seed packet.\n2. **Create a catalog**: Consider creating a list or flyers of the seeds donated by category. This will help attendees quickly scan through the offerings and identify what's available.\n3. **Use a tracking system**: To keep track of seeds that are being swapped, consider creating a wish list or a sign-in sheet for attendees to indicate which seeds they'd like to take. This can help prevent seeds from being \"lost in the process.\"\n\n**During the Seed Swap**\n\n1. **Have plenty of volunteers on hand**: Assign a few volunteers to help manage the tables, answer questions, and facilitate the seed swap process.\n2. **Welcome and introduce yourself**: Take a few minutes to explain the seed swap rules, any specific requirements or restrictions (e.g., clean packaging, quantity limits), and encourage attendees to engage with one another.\n3. **Allow time for browsing**: Give attendees time to browse through the seeds and interact with one another.\n\n**Tips and Reminders**\n\n1. **Encourage clean packaging**: Remind attendees to clean and label their seed packets before dropping them off at the seed sort.\n2. **Set a limit on the number of seeds per attendee**: This will help prevent one person from taking an inordinate amount of seeds and ensure the swap remains accessible to all.\n3. **Have extra materials on hand**: Bring small bags or containers for attendees to fill with their new seeds, as well as markers and other handy items.\n\nRemember, the key to a successful seed swap is clear communication, organization, and a welcoming atmosphere. Encourage attendees to get involved, share knowledge, and have fun!", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
+{"item_id": "effort_0113", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I need a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store. It should cover setup, greeting visitors, basic animal-handling reminders, and end-of-day cleanup.", "answer": "Here's a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store:\n\n**Setup (before event starts)**\n\n* Arrive 30 minutes before the event to help set up: tables, banners, and cat enclosures\n* Ensure all cats are comfortable and securely housed\n* Review the event schedule, volunteer roles, and contact information for staff and management\n* Familiarize yourself with the pet store's procedures and emergency protocols\n\n**Greeting Visitors**\n\n* Welcome potential adopters and answer questions about the cats and adoption process\n* Ensure all visitors are aware of the adoption process, including:\n\t+ Spay/neuter requirements\n\t+ Vaccination records\n\t+ Adoption contracts\n\t+ Post-adoption support\n* Be prepared to introduce visitors to the cats and help facilitate interactions\n\n**Basic Animal-Handling Reminders**\n\n* Always wash your hands before and after handling the cats\n* Handle the cats gently and humanely, supporting their body and lifting them carefully\n* Avoid startling or overwhelming the cats, especially those who are shy or fearful\n* Keep the cats' enclosures clean and well-ventilated to prevent stress and illness\n* Be respectful of each cat's individual personality and boundaries\n\n**End-of-Day Cleanup (after event)**\n\n* Help remove all equipment, displays, and banners\n* Disinfect all surfaces and equipment used during the event\n* Ensure all cats are safely secured and comfortable in their enclosures\n* Review any event feedback or concerns with staff and management\n* Complete any necessary paperwork or reporting requirements\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Be prepared to work in a dynamic environment with potential adopters, staff, and volunteers\n* Stay calm and patient, even in high-pressure situations\n* Have fun and enjoy interacting with the cats and visitors!\n\nRemember to review the event schedule and volunteer roles with your team before the event, and have a plan in place for emergencies or unexpected situations. Thank you for volunteering your time to help the cats find forever homes!", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0102", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m helping organize a community art fair and need a simple way to assign booth numbers to vendors so setup day is less chaotic. Can you suggest an easy numbering system and a short instruction note we can send vendors?", "answer": "Assigning booth numbers in advance can indeed make a big difference in setup day's chaos-level. Here's a simple suggested numbering system and a sample instruction note:\n\n**Suggested Numbering System:**\n\n1. Divide the art fair area into sections or zones, such as alphabetical (A, B, C), numerical (1, 2, 3), or geographic (East, West, Center).\n2. Assign vendors to each section/zone, and then assign a specific booth number within that section.\n3. For example:\n\t* Section A (Artisans A-Z): 1-10 (A1, A2, A3,..., A10)\n\t* Section B (Bakeries 1-5): 1-5 (B1, B2,..., B5)\n\t* Zone 3 (Center): 1-10 (C1, C2,..., C10)\n\n**Sample Instruction Note:**\n\n**Vendor Assignment Information**\n\nTo expedite setup on [Date], please refer to your assigned booth number and locate your designated area. Your booth number is:\n\n Booth Number: _____________________ (insert section/zone and number, e.g., A5 or B2)\n\n Location: _____________________________________\n\n Section/Zone: _______________________________\n\n/Employees' guide to setup will be available at the registration desk on the day of the event.\n\n**Instructions:**\n\n* Arrive at least 60 minutes prior to the designated setup time to gain access to your booth location\n* Follow signage to your assigned booth area\n* Set up your booth according to the provided layout and guidelines\n* Bring any necessary materials, tools, or supplies for setup\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to contact us at [Your email or phone number].", "judge_response": "74", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 74}
+{"item_id": "effort_0089", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I volunteer at a neighborhood tool library and need a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools before we put them into circulation. Can you make a practical checklist that non-experts could use?", "answer": "A well-organized inspection checklist can help ensure that the tools in your neighborhood tool library are safe and functional for borrowers. Here's a simple, non-technical checklist that non-experts can use:\n\n**Tool Inspection Checklist**\n\n**General**\n\n1. **Overall condition**: Is the tool clean and free of significant rust, corrosion, or debris?\n2. **Labeling**: Is the tool properly labeled with its type, function, and any relevant instructions?\n3. **Storage**: Is the tool stored properly in a secure, dry area to prevent loss or damage?\n\n**Handle and Grip**\n\n1. **Grip**: Is the grip comfortable to hold, and free of cracks, breaks, or excessive wear?\n2. **Handle attachment**: Is the handle securely attached to the tool, and not loose or wobbly?\n\n**Tool Head and Blades**\n\n1. **Blade/Bit condition**: Is the blade or bit sharp, and not damaged or excessively worn?\n2. **Blade/Bit location**: Is the blade or bit securely attached to the tool, and not loose?\n3. **Rust or corrosion**: Are there any signs of rust or corrosion on the tool head or blades?\n\n**Moving Parts**\n\n1. **Smooth operation**: Do the tool's moving parts (e.g., pivot points, levers) move smoothly and freely?\n2. **Secure retention**: Are parts that should be locked (e.g., pegs, screws) securely retained?\n\n**Idenity and Certification**\n\n1. **Certification**: Is the tool marked as safe for use (e.g., ANSI certified)?\n2. **Brand or model identification**: Is the tool's brand and model clearly identified?\n\n**Other**\n\n1. **Missing parts**: Are there any missing parts or components necessary for safe use?\n2. **Safety warnings**: Are there any obvious safety concerns or warnings associated with the tool (e.g., warning labels, cautions)?\n\n**Passing Criteria**\n\n* The tool meets the above criteria and is deemed safe for use.\n* If you're unsure about any aspect of the tool, do not inspect it, or refer it to a more qualified person.\n\n**Actions**\n\n* **Accept**: If the tool passes inspection, it's ready for borrowing.\n* **Decline**: If the tool fails inspection, do not borrow it, and close inspection comments to note why it was declined.", "judge_response": "84", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 84}
+{"item_id": "effort_0112", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m putting together a simple orientation packet for a volunteer who will water and do quick visual checks on the plants in our small office during a three-week summer break. Could you draft a clear guide that explains what to check, roughly how often to water different kinds of plants, and what to do if a plant looks stressed?", "answer": "Here's a draft guide for your volunteer:\n\n**Welcome to Plant Care!**\n\nThank you for volunteering to help care for our office plants during the summer break. Your attention to detail will help ensure our plants stay healthy and thrive while we're away. This guide will walk you through what to check, how often to water, and what to do if you notice any issues.\n\n**What to Check:**\n\n* All plants: Look for signs of stress, such as:\n\t+ Wilting or droopy leaves\n\t+ Yellowing or browning leaves\n\t+ Soft or mushy leaves\n\t+ Dry soil or cracked pot\n* Check the temperature and humidity in the office:\n\t+ Make sure the temperature is within the acceptable range for each plant (most plants prefer daytime temperatures between 65-75\u00b0F and nighttime temperatures around 60-70\u00b0F)\n\t+ Check for any drafts or extreme temperature fluctuations\n\n**Watering Schedule:**\n\n* **Low-Maintenance Plants:** Water every 7-10 days, such as:\n\t+ Snake Plants (Sansevieria)\n\t+ ZZ Plant (Zamioculcas zamiifolia)\n\t+ Pothos (Epipremnum aureum)\n* **Medium-Maintenance Plants:** Water every 5-7 days, such as:\n\t+ Dracaena (Dracaena spp.)\n\t+ Philodendron (Philodendron spp.)\n\t+ Peace Lily (Spathiphyllum wallisii)\n* **High-Maintenance Plants:** Water every 3-5 days, such as:\n\t+ Ferns (Various species)\n\t+ Boston Ferns (Nephrolepis exaltata)\n\n**How to Water:**\n\n* Check the soil moisture by sticking your finger into the soil up to the first knuckle. If the soil feels dry, it's time to water.\n* Water at room temperature using lukewarm water.\n* Water thoroughly until you see water coming out of the drainage holes.\n* Avoid getting water on the leaves or crown of the plant to prevent rot and disease.\n\n**What to Do if a Plant Looks Stressed:**\n\n* **Wilting:** Check that the plant is receiving enough water and adjust watering schedule as needed.\n* **Yellowing or Browning Leaves:** Check for overwatering, underwatering, or nutrient deficiencies. Prune affected leaves to prevent the spread of disease.\n* **Soft or Mushy Leaves:** This may indicate overwatering. Check the soil moisture and adjust watering schedule. If the issue persists, consider repotting the plant in fresh soil.\n* **Dry Soil or Cracked Pot:** Increase watering frequency or repot the plant in a more forgiving potting mix.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* If you notice any pests, such as spider mites, mealybugs, or aphids, please notify our team so we can take action to address the issue.\n* Keep plants away from direct sunlight, heating vents, and air conditioning units.\n* Avoid fertilizing plants during the summer break, as this can stimulate new growth and make them more susceptible to stress.\n\nRemember to ask our team if you have any questions or concerns about a specific plant. We'll be happy to provide more guidance.\n\nThank you for helping us keep our office plants happy and healthy!", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
