+{"item_id": "effort_0104", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m trying to build a basic cleaning checklist for my car so I can do a quick reset once a month. Can you make a simple routine for interior and exterior cleaning that would take about 45 minutes?", "answer": "Here's a 45-minute cleaning routine for your car's interior and exterior:\n\n**Exterior (15 minutes)**\n\n1. **Rinse and remove loose debris**: Quickly rinse the car with a hose to remove any loose dirt or debris.\n2. **Wipe down exterior**: Use a microfiber cloth to wipe down the entire exterior of the car, including the hood, trunk, and doors.\n3. **Clean wheel wells and tires**: Use a separate cloth to clean the wheel wells and tires.\n4. **Glass cleaning**: Use a glass cleaner and a microfiber cloth to clean the windshield, side windows, and rear window.\n\n**Interior (30 minutes)**\n\n1. **Quick vacuum**: Use a handheld vacuum or a portable vacuum to quickly remove any loose dirt, dust, or debris from the interior.\n2. **Wipe down interior surfaces**: Use a microfiber cloth to wipe down the dashboard, center console, and door panels.\n3. **Clean door handles and knobs**: Use a disinfectant wipe to clean the door handles and knobs.\n4. **Vacuum seats**: Use the handheld vacuum or portable vacuum to quickly vacuum the seats.\n5. **Clean floormats and carpets**: Use a small brush or a carpet cleaner to gently clean the floormats and carpets.\n6. **Dust and declutter**: Take a few minutes to dust any remaining surfaces, like the air vents or trim, and quickly tidy up any clutter.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Use microfiber cloths to avoid scratching surfaces and make cleaning easier.\n* Use cleaning products specifically designed for cars to avoid damaging surfaces.\n* If you have any stubborn stains or odors, consider setting aside more time for a deeper clean.\n* Consider performing a more thorough interior clean every 3-6 months, depending on your usage and preferences.\n\nRemember, this is a basic routine and can be adjusted to fit your specific needs and car.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0113", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I need a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store. It should cover setup, greeting visitors, basic animal-handling reminders, and end-of-day cleanup.", "answer": "Here's a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store:\n\n**Pre-Event Checklist**\n\n1. **Arrival and Setup** (30 minutes before event start)\n\t* Arrive at the pet store and meet with other volunteers and event staff\n\t* Review event schedule and responsibilities\n\t* Set up cat play areas, food and water stations, and adoption tables\n\t* Ensure all necessary materials (e.g. litter boxes, toys, adoption paperwork) are available\n2. **Greeting Visitors** (event duration)\n\t* Greet visitors warmly and welcome them to the adoption event\n\t* Ask if they're interested in learning about the cats or adopting a cat\n\t* Provide information about the cats available for adoption (e.g. breed, age, personality traits)\n\t* Assist with adoption applications and answer questions about the adoption process\n\n**Animal-Handling Reminders**\n\n1. **Wash Your Hands**: Wash your hands frequently before and after handling the cats\n2. **Check for Fright or Stress**: Look for signs of fear or stress in the cats (e.g. hiding, hissing, avoiding contact)\n3. **Support Cat Carriers**: Offer support and assistance when lifting or carrying cat carriers\n4. **Respect Cats' Boundaries**: Let cats set the pace and initiate contact; don't pick up or handle cats that are uninterested\n\n**End-of-Day Checklist**\n\n1. **Clean Up**: Help clean and disinfect all areas used by the cats, including food and water stations, litter boxes, and play areas\n2. **Disassemble Cat Play Areas**: Remove cat play areas, toys, and other setup materials from the store\n3. **Secure Cats**: Ensure all cats are secured in their carriers and ready for transport to a foster home or other safe location\n4. **Review Day's Events**: Debrief with other volunteers and event staff to discuss successes, challenges, and areas for improvement for future events\n\nRemember to also review any specific store policies or procedures before the event, and ask event staff or volunteers with experience if you have any questions or concerns. Good luck with the event!", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "Creating a simple and consistent labeling system for your community theater booth will help volunteers stay organized and efficient. Here's a suggested system that works for audio, lighting, and power items:\n\n**Label Structure**\n\nUse a combination of descriptive words to create a unique and readable label for each item. The structure can be:\n\n`[Location] - [Equipment Type] - [Device/Function] - [Identifier]`\n\nbroken down into:\n\n1. **Location**: Where the device is located (e.g., sound console, stage left, lighting grid)\n2. **Equipment Type**: General category of the device (e.g., audio, lighting, power)\n3. **Device/Function**: Specific description of the device or its function (e.g., mixing board, dimmer pack, extension cord)\n4. **Identifier**: Unique identifier for the device (e.g., \"Layer 1\", \" Channel 12\", \"Branch 3\")\n\n**Example Labels**\n\n1. \"Stage Left - Audio - Microphone - Stage Left Mic\"\n2. \"Lighting Grid - Dimmer Pack - Branch 3\"\n3. \"Sound Console - Mixing Board - Channel 12\"\n4. \"Power Strip - Extension Cord - 20A\"\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Use a consistent font and size for all labels to ensure readability.\n* Consider using a colorcoding system (e.g., red for critical items, green for less critical ones) to draw attention to important devices.\n* Include relevant icons or images to help volunteers quickly identify the device's function.\n* Make sure to label cables and connectors as well, using the same structure and additional information (e.g., \"Stage Left - Audio - XLR Cable - Channel 5\").\n* Consider creating a master list or diagram of the equipment and setup, in case volunteers need further reference.\n\n**Field Adaptation**\n\nSince you're working in a specific context, you might need to adapt this system to fit your theater's specific requirements. Some questions to consider:\n\n* Do you have specific terminology or conventions for your equipment?\n* Are there any critical devices or systems that require special attention?\n* Are there any connectivity options (e.g., USB, Ethernet) that require additional labeling?\n\nCustomize the system to fit your needs, and make sure to communicate it clearly to your volunteers. A well-organized labeling system will make a significant difference in reducing setup time and minimizing errors.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0102", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m helping organize a community art fair and need a simple way to assign booth numbers to vendors so setup day is less chaotic. Can you suggest an easy numbering system and a short instruction note we can send vendors?", "answer": "Here's a simple numbering system and a short instruction note you can send to vendors:\n\n**Numbering System:**\n\nTo assign booth numbers, simply number the booths in a grid or linear fashion, starting from a neutral corner (e.g. top left). For example:\n\nGrid System:\n1-10 (top row), 11-20 (center row), 21-30 (bottom row)\n11-20 (left column), 21-30 (center column), 31-40 (right column)\n\nLinear System:\n1-40 (in numerical order along one long axis, with the first number closest to the entrance)\n\n**Instruction Note:**\n\nSubject: Important: Booth Numbering and Setup Instructions for [Event Name]\n\nDear Vendors,\n\nWe're excited to have you participate in our community art fair! To ensure a smooth setup process, we've assigned booth numbers to each of you. Please find your assigned number below and use it to mark your display. You can also use the attached map to locate your space.\n\n**Booth Numbering:**\n[Insert numeric description of your assigned system above]\n\n**Setup Instructions:**\n\n* Please arrive between [insert timeframe] to set up your display.\n* Set up on the assigned booth number and be sure to check in with our team to confirm your exhibit is correctly labeled.\n* We encourage you to bring your own equipment, display materials, and any electrical needs (check our event website for options).\n* A limited number of tables and chairs are provided, but please notify us in advance if you require additional materials.\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to reach out. We're looking forward to a wonderful art fair and appreciate your participation!\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name/Event Team]\n\nFeel free to customize the instruction note to fit your event's specific needs and time frame.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
