+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "Creating a simple and consistent labeling system for your community theater booth will help volunteers stay organized and efficient. Here's a suggested system that works for audio, lighting, and power items:\n\n**Label Structure**\n\nUse a combination of descriptive words to create a unique and readable label for each item. The structure can be:\n\n`[Location] - [Equipment Type] - [Device/Function] - [Identifier]`\n\nbroken down into:\n\n1. **Location**: Where the device is located (e.g., sound console, stage left, lighting grid)\n2. **Equipment Type**: General category of the device (e.g., audio, lighting, power)\n3. **Device/Function**: Specific description of the device or its function (e.g., mixing board, dimmer pack, extension cord)\n4. **Identifier**: Unique identifier for the device (e.g., \"Layer 1\", \" Channel 12\", \"Branch 3\")\n\n**Example Labels**\n\n1. \"Stage Left - Audio - Microphone - Stage Left Mic\"\n2. \"Lighting Grid - Dimmer Pack - Branch 3\"\n3. \"Sound Console - Mixing Board - Channel 12\"\n4. \"Power Strip - Extension Cord - 20A\"\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Use a consistent font and size for all labels to ensure readability.\n* Consider using a colorcoding system (e.g., red for critical items, green for less critical ones) to draw attention to important devices.\n* Include relevant icons or images to help volunteers quickly identify the device's function.\n* Make sure to label cables and connectors as well, using the same structure and additional information (e.g., \"Stage Left - Audio - XLR Cable - Channel 5\").\n* Consider creating a master list or diagram of the equipment and setup, in case volunteers need further reference.\n\n**Field Adaptation**\n\nSince you're working in a specific context, you might need to adapt this system to fit your theater's specific requirements. Some questions to consider:\n\n* Do you have specific terminology or conventions for your equipment?\n* Are there any critical devices or systems that require special attention?\n* Are there any connectivity options (e.g., USB, Ethernet) that require additional labeling?\n\nCustomize the system to fit your needs, and make sure to communicate it clearly to your volunteers. A well-organized labeling system will make a significant difference in reducing setup time and minimizing errors.", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0116", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m hosting a neighborhood seed swap next month. Can you help me make a simple plan for how to set up the tables, label the seeds, and keep the exchange organized?", "answer": "A neighborhood seed swap sounds like a lovely community-building event. Here's a simple plan to help you set up and run a successful seed swap:\n\n**Table Setup:**\n\n1. **Seed Table:** Designate a long table (e.g., 6-8 feet) for the seed exchange. This will be the main table where neighbors will display and exchange seeds.\n2. **Categories Table:** Set up a smaller table or a shelf near the seed table to organize seeds by category (e.g., flowers, vegetables, herbs, native plants).\n3. **Seed Sign-up Table:** Create a separate table or a clipboard for neighbors to sign up to participate in the swap, so you can get an estimate of the number of participants.\n4. **Information Table:** Consider setting up a small table or a corner with information about seed saving, local gardening resources, and any relevant neighborhood gardening initiatives.\n\n**Labeling Seeds:**\n\n1. **Seed Envelopes or Bags:** Provide small envelopes or bags (like paper bags or 2-inch square envelopes) for neighbors to store and display their seeds.\n2. **Seed Labels:** Prepare a set of labels or stickers with a simple format:\n\t* Seed Name (as printed on the package or the packet)\n\t* Category (e.g., flowers, vegetables, herbs)\n\t* Seed Quantity (e.g., # seeds per package)\n3. **Seed Signs:** Create a simple sign or display with space for neighbors to write their name, seed name, and any additional information (e.g., special instructions or growing tips).\n\n**Keeping the Exchange Organized:**\n\n1. **Seed Registration:** Before the swap, have participants register their seeds by filling out a sign-up sheet or online form. This will help you estimate the number of participants and plan accordingly.\n2. **Seed Display:** Suggest that neighbors display their seeds in the designated area, using the seed envelopes or bags and labeling them according to the format above.\n3. **Seed Table Organization:** Set up a system to keep the seeds organized, such as by category or alphabetically by seed name.\n4. **Exchange Process:** Plan a straightforward exchange process:\n\t* Participants browse the seed table and select the seeds they want to take.\n\t* Each participant contributes a set number of seeds (e.g., 10 seeds) to the table.\n\t* The host distributes a free list of seeds available for pick-up (optional).\n\t* Participants take their selected seeds and contribute their own seeds to the table.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n1. **Promotion:** Advertise the seed swap through social media, neighborhood newsletters, and door-to-door flyers.\n2. **Seed Quality:** Encourage participants to bring healthy, disease-free seeds.\n3. **Communal Seed Bank:** Consider creating a small seed bank with a mix of seeds for anyone to use in future garden growth.\n4. **Follow-up:** Send a thank-you note or survey after the event to gather feedback and encourage continued gardening community engagement.\n\nThis plan provides a basic structure for a successful seed swap. Feel free to adapt and add details as you see fit to make the event more engaging and enjoyable for your neighborhood gardeners. Happy seed swapping!", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0113", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I need a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store. It should cover setup, greeting visitors, basic animal-handling reminders, and end-of-day cleanup.", "answer": "Here's a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store:\n\n**Pre-Event Checklist**\n\n1. **Arrival and Setup** (30 minutes before event start)\n\t* Arrive at the pet store and meet with other volunteers and event staff\n\t* Review event schedule and responsibilities\n\t* Set up cat play areas, food and water stations, and adoption tables\n\t* Ensure all necessary materials (e.g. litter boxes, toys, adoption paperwork) are available\n2. **Greeting Visitors** (event duration)\n\t* Greet visitors warmly and welcome them to the adoption event\n\t* Ask if they're interested in learning about the cats or adopting a cat\n\t* Provide information about the cats available for adoption (e.g. breed, age, personality traits)\n\t* Assist with adoption applications and answer questions about the adoption process\n\n**Animal-Handling Reminders**\n\n1. **Wash Your Hands**: Wash your hands frequently before and after handling the cats\n2. **Check for Fright or Stress**: Look for signs of fear or stress in the cats (e.g. hiding, hissing, avoiding contact)\n3. **Support Cat Carriers**: Offer support and assistance when lifting or carrying cat carriers\n4. **Respect Cats' Boundaries**: Let cats set the pace and initiate contact; don't pick up or handle cats that are uninterested\n\n**End-of-Day Checklist**\n\n1. **Clean Up**: Help clean and disinfect all areas used by the cats, including food and water stations, litter boxes, and play areas\n2. **Disassemble Cat Play Areas**: Remove cat play areas, toys, and other setup materials from the store\n3. **Secure Cats**: Ensure all cats are secured in their carriers and ready for transport to a foster home or other safe location\n4. **Review Day's Events**: Debrief with other volunteers and event staff to discuss successes, challenges, and areas for improvement for future events\n\nRemember to also review any specific store policies or procedures before the event, and ask event staff or volunteers with experience if you have any questions or concerns. Good luck with the event!", "judge_response": "72", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 72}
+{"item_id": "effort_0102", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m helping organize a community art fair and need a simple way to assign booth numbers to vendors so setup day is less chaotic. Can you suggest an easy numbering system and a short instruction note we can send vendors?", "answer": "Here's a simple numbering system and a short instruction note you can send to vendors:\n\n**Numbering System:**\n\nTo assign booth numbers, simply number the booths in a grid or linear fashion, starting from a neutral corner (e.g. top left). For example:\n\nGrid System:\n1-10 (top row), 11-20 (center row), 21-30 (bottom row)\n11-20 (left column), 21-30 (center column), 31-40 (right column)\n\nLinear System:\n1-40 (in numerical order along one long axis, with the first number closest to the entrance)\n\n**Instruction Note:**\n\nSubject: Important: Booth Numbering and Setup Instructions for [Event Name]\n\nDear Vendors,\n\nWe're excited to have you participate in our community art fair! To ensure a smooth setup process, we've assigned booth numbers to each of you. Please find your assigned number below and use it to mark your display. You can also use the attached map to locate your space.\n\n**Booth Numbering:**\n[Insert numeric description of your assigned system above]\n\n**Setup Instructions:**\n\n* Please arrive between [insert timeframe] to set up your display.\n* Set up on the assigned booth number and be sure to check in with our team to confirm your exhibit is correctly labeled.\n* We encourage you to bring your own equipment, display materials, and any electrical needs (check our event website for options).\n* A limited number of tables and chairs are provided, but please notify us in advance if you require additional materials.\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to reach out. We're looking forward to a wonderful art fair and appreciate your participation!\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name/Event Team]\n\nFeel free to customize the instruction note to fit your event's specific needs and time frame.", "judge_response": "76", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 76}
+{"item_id": "effort_0089", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I volunteer at a neighborhood tool library and need a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools before we put them into circulation. Can you make a practical checklist that non-experts could use?", "answer": "A tool library inspection checklist is essential to ensure the tools are safe, functional, and maintained properly. Here's a simple and practical checklist you can adapt:\n\n**Tool Inspection Checklist:**\n\n**General Condition**\n\n1. Tool is complete and includes all necessary parts (e.g., handle, blade, bits, etc.)\n2. Tool appears to be in good condition, with no major signs of rust, corrosion, or damage\n3. Tool feels sturdy and well-built\n\n**Safety Features**\n\n1. Sharp edges and points are guarded or covered (e.g., on saw blades, chisels, etc.)\n2. Hole centers and screw drive slots are aligned and secure\n3. Handles are securely attached to the tool head or shaft\n\n**Functionality**\n\n1. Tool operates smoothly and makes required movements (e.g., tightening, loosening, etc.)\n2. All handles (e.g., grips, knobs) turn or twist freely without sticks or binding\n3. Tool has a secure locking mechanism (if applicable)\n\n**I Don't Know (Red Flag)**\n\nIf unsure about any aspect of the tool, DO NOT circulate it. Instead, mark it as a \"I Don't Know\" and either:\n\n* Set the tool aside for further review or repair\n* Add it to a \"Heritage\" or \"Rebuild\" box, where it can be re-examined or repaired in the future\n* Document the tool's condition and add it to a wish list for donations to replace or upgrade it\n\n**Specific Tool Checklists (adapt to your collection):**\n\n* **Drills and Drivers**: Check for loose or damaged bits, inspect drill bits for worn or damaged edges, and ensure driver's socket sizes are accurate\n* **Saws**: Check for worn or damaged saw blades, and ensure the saw's tension is properly set\n* **Hand Files and Sandpaper**: Inspect for worn or frayed surfaces, and ensure files are properly seated and free-moving\n* **Pliers and Wrenches**: Check for loose or damaged handles, and ensure wrenches are properly seated and calibrated\n* **Tool Sets**: Inspect for matching and completeness, check for any missing tools, and verify set organizers are functional\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Document your inspection process, including any issues or repairs made, in the tool's catalog entry or file.\n* Consider inviting a local expert (e.g., handyman, contractor) to review specific tools, especially if you're unsure about an item's condition or safety.\n* Use color-coding or stickers to indicate which tools have been inspected and are ready for circulation.\n\nBy using this checklist, your neighborhood tool library volunteers can ensure donated hand tools are safe, functional, and well-maintained for the community's use. Happy tool organizing!", "judge_response": "84", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 84}
+{"item_id": "effort_0112", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "metric": "effort_score", "question": "I\u2019m putting together a simple orientation packet for a volunteer who will water and do quick visual checks on the plants in our small office during a three-week summer break. Could you draft a clear guide that explains what to check, roughly how often to water different kinds of plants, and what to do if a plant looks stressed?", "answer": "Here's a draft of a concise and clear orientation packet for your volunteer:\n\n**Welcome to Our Summer Plant Care Team!**\n\nWe're grateful to have you help us care for our office plants during the summer break. Our goal is to provide a welcoming and healthy environment for everyone, and with your help, we can keep our plants thriving.\n\n**What to Check**\n\nPlease take a few minutes each week to walk around the office and do a quick visual check on all the plants. Look for:\n\n* Water levels: Make sure the soil isn't too dry or waterlogged.\n* Leaf health: Check for signs of pests, disease, or stress.\n* Temperature: Ensure the plants are not exposed to direct sunlight, extreme temperatures, or drafts.\n* Humidity: Some plants prefer a more humid environment; keep an eye out for plants that may be dropping leaves or showing signs of stress.\n\n**Watering Schedule**\n\nPlease follow this rough guideline for watering different kinds of plants:\n\n* **Tropical plants** (e.g., peace lilies, spider plants, ferns): Water every 7-10 days. These plants prefer consistently moist soil.\n* **Succulents** (e.g., aloe, echeveria, cacti): Water every 10-14 days. Allow the soil to dry out completely between waterings.\n* **Herbs** (e.g., basil, mint, rosemary): Water every 5-7 days. Herbs require consistent moisture to thrive.\n* **Low-maintenance plants** (e.g., snake plants, ZZ plants, air plants): Water every 10-14 days. These plants are forgiving if you forget to water them from time to time.\n\n**What to Do if a Plant Looks Stressed**\n\nIf a plant is showing signs of stress, such as:\n\n* Yellow or dropping leaves\n* Wilting or soft stems\n* White, cottony growth on leaves\n* Small, curled, or distorted leaves\n\nCheck the soil moisture and adjust your watering schedule as needed. If the issue persists, consider:\n\n* Talking to us about adjusting the plant's location or providing more humidity.\n* Pruning or repotting the plant to promote new growth.\n* Giving the plant a gentle misting with a spray bottle (be cautious not to overwater).\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* If you notice any pests (e.g., mealybugs, spider mites, scale), alert us immediately so we can address the issue.\n* Keep an eye out for overwatered plants, which can develop root rot.\n* Use a water-absorbing tray or saucer under pot-bound plants to prevent water from pooling and causing root rot.\n\n**Questions?**\n\nDon't hesitate to reach out to us if you have any questions or concerns about our plants. We're here to help and appreciate your care!\n\nThank you for your help in keeping our office plants happy and healthy!", "judge_response": "78", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 78}
