+{"item_id": "effort_0084", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m labeling cables and small devices in a community theater booth so volunteers can set things up without guessing. Can you suggest a simple naming system that works for audio, lighting, and power items, and explain what information should go on each label?", "answer": "Here's a simple naming system that can help you label your cables and devices in a clear and efficient manner:\n\n**Label Format:**\n```\n[Device Type] - [Location/Room/Area] - [Specific Device/Object] - [Function/Usage]\n```\n\n**Breakdown:**\n\n* **Device Type:**\n\t+ Audio: MIC (microphone), SPEAKER (speaker), EQ (equalizer), AMP (amplifier), etc.\n\t+ Lighting: LAMP (lighting fixture), DMX (DMX controller), STAND (lighting stand), etc.\n\t+ Power: POWER (UPS, power strip), OUTLET (outdoor outlet), FAN (ventilation fan), etc.\n* **Location/Room/Area:**\n\t+ Theater name, or specific area (e.g., \"Main Stage\", \"Backstage\", \"Green Room\")\n\t+ Abbreviations can be used for common locations\n* **Specific Device/Object:**\n\t+ Model number, name, or a brief description (e.g., \"Condenser Mic\", \"Stage Right Speaker\", \" DMX Console\")\n* **Function/Usage:**\n\t+ Describe the device's primary function (e.g., \"Wired for Sound,\" \"Strobe Warning,\" \" Stage Power\")\n\n**Example Labels:**\n\n* Audio:\n\t+ \"MIC - Main Stage - Condenser Mic - Wired for Sound\"\n\t+ \"SPEAKER - Backstage - Stage Left Speaker - Playback\"\n* Lighting:\n\t+ \"LAMP - Stage Area - Front Of House Spotlight - Cue Canopy\"\n\t+ \"DMX - Lighting Con - DMX Console - Control\"\n* Power:\n\t+ \"POWER - Outer Building - 20A UPS - Building Power\"\n\t+ \"OUTLET - Rear Wing - Wet Outlet - Outdoor Stage Power\"\n\n**Tips:**\n\n* Keep labels concise and clear.\n* Use a standard font and size for readability.\n* Consider using a summary label for groups of similar devices, like \"Audio Cables - Main Stage\" and detailed labels for each specific cable.\n* Make sure to include necessary safety information, such as hazard warnings or emergency shutdown procedures, if applicable.\n\nBy following this system, your labels should be easy to read and understand for volunteers, ensuring a smooth setup and operation of your community theater booth.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0104", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m trying to build a basic cleaning checklist for my car so I can do a quick reset once a month. Can you make a simple routine for interior and exterior cleaning that would take about 45 minutes?", "answer": "Here's a simple 45-minute checklist for a monthly car cleaning reset:\n\n**Exterior (20-25 minutes)**\n\n1. **Start with a water rinse** (5 minutes):\n\t* Quickly rinse the car to remove loose dirt and debris.\n2. **Wash the windows and mirrors** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a glass cleaner and a microfiber cloth to clean all windows, mirrors, and any other glass surfaces.\n3. **Clean the exterior trim and moldings** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a soft cloth and a gentle cleaner to wipe down exterior trim, moldings, and wheel wells.\n4. **Wipe down the wheels and tires** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a soft cloth and a gentle cleaner to wipe down the wheels and tires.\n5. **Dry and inspect** (5 minutes):\n\t* Dry the car with a microfiber cloth to prevent water spots.\n\t* Inspect the exterior for any remaining dirt or debris.\n\n**Interior (20-25 minutes)**\n\n1. **Remove trash and loose items** (5 minutes):\n\t* Get rid of any trash, empty water bottles, or other loose items.\n2. **Vacuum the interior** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a handheld vacuum or a car vacuum cleaner to quickly clean the seats, floor mats, and any other surfaces.\n3. **Clean the dashboard and console** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a gentle cleaner and a soft cloth to wipe down the dashboard and console.\n4. **Clean the seats** (5 minutes):\n\t* Use a gentle cleaner and a soft cloth to wipe down the seats.\n5. **Quickly tidy up** (5 minutes):\n\t* Straighten up any thrown-away items and make sure the interior is generally tidy.\n\n**Additional tips:**\n\n* Use a microfiber cloth to clean most surfaces, as they are gentle and effective.\n* Use a gentle cleaner to clean any sensitive surfaces, like the dashboard or console.\n* Don't forget to clean the door handles, center console, and any other high-touch areas.\n* If you have a particularly dirty car, you may want to add an extra 10-15 minutes to this routine.\n\nRemember, this is a quick reset routine, so it's meant to be a surface-level cleaning. If you have specific concerns or messes, you may want to add additional tasks or products to your routine. Happy cleaning!", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0100", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "Can you give me a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for my department? Just the basics is fine.", "answer": "Here's a compact plan for setting up a recurring monthly report for your department:\n\n**Pre-Reporting Cycle (Days 1-5)**\n\n1. **Define report purpose**: Clearly outline the goals and objectives of the report.\n2. **Gather data sources**: Identify the necessary data sources and metrics to be included in the report.\n3. **Design report layout**: Determine the report's format, including the structure, tables, charts, and other visual elements.\n4. **Develop a content calendar**: Plan the report's content and tone for the upcoming month.\n\n**Reporting Cycle (Days 6-28)**\n\n1. **Collect data**: Gather data from identified sources and update the report as necessary.\n2. **Create draft report**: Construct the report using the designed layout and gathered data.\n3. **Review and edit**: Review the report for accuracy, completeness, and coherence.\n4. **Obtain approval**: Get approval from stakeholders and audience before finalizing the report.\n\n**Post-Reporting Cycle (Days 29-30)**\n\n1. **Publish report**: Distribute the report to the intended audience on the designated day of the month.\n2. **Monitor feedback**: Solicit feedback from stakeholders and incorporate suggestions for future improvements.\n3. **File and archive**: Store the report in a secure location for future reference.\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Establish a deadline for report submission to the department head or other senior manager.\n* Consider automation tools to streamline the reporting process and save time.\n* Develop a process for handling late reports or special requests.\n* Rotate report responsibilities among team members to promote collaboration and learning.\n\nRemember to adjust this plan according to your department's specific needs, size, and complexity.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0056", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m making a simple check-in process for a volunteer-run repair caf\u00e9 where people bring in broken household items. Can you suggest an easy intake form and a basic flow so arrivals don\u2019t get confusing?", "answer": "Here are some suggestions for an easy intake form and a basic flow to help manage the check-in process:\n\n**Intake Form:**\n\nConsider using a simple, one-page form that captures the following information:\n\n1. **Item Details**\n\t* Type of item (e.g., appliance, furniture, electronics, etc.)\n\t* Description of the item (e.g., toaster, chair, laptop, etc.)\n2. **Breakdown Description**\n\t* What's broken/doesn't work?\n\t* Any error messages or symptoms?\n3. **History**\n\t* How long has the item been broken?\n\t* Has it been repaired/attempted previously?\n4. **Pre-Repair Warnings**\n\t* Are there any safety concerns or hazards associated with the item?\n\t* Are there any specific skills or tools required to fix it?\n5. **Contact Information**\n\t* Name and contact phone number (optional)\n\t* Email (optional)\n\n**Basic Flow:**\n\nTo streamline the check-in process, consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Initial Contact**:\n\t* Greet arrivals warmly and ask if they have an item to bring in for repair.\n\t* If yes, ask if they have the intake form ready or would like to take one.\n2. **Check-in and Form Filling**:\n\t* Have a designated volunteer responsible for taking the intake form and asking the questions.\n\t* Assist volunteers (if needed) and provide necessary guidance.\n3. **Sorting and Assessment**:\n\t* After completing the form, ask the volunteer running the repair to quickly assess the item's feasibility for repair.\n\t* If it's unclear, ask the volunteer to consult with a more experienced team member.\n4. **Waitlist Creation**:\n\t* If the item requires a specific skill or tool to repair, create a waitlist to ensure the right volunteer is assigned.\n\t* This will help manage volunteer availability and minimize wait times.\n5. **Item Processing**:\n\t* Once the item is deemed suitable for repair, assign a volunteer and schedule a repair time.\n\t* Ensure the volunteer understands the breakdown description, any safety concerns, and the expected repair duration.\n6. **Update and Follow-up**:\n\t* Communicate with the item's owner about the repair status via phone or email (if provided).\n\t* Update the waitlist and volunteer assignments as necessary.\n\n**Additional Tips:**\n\n* Consider using a clipboard with a pen and paper for the intake form, or a digital version with a mobile device (e.g., tablet).\n* Place a simple, easily understandable sign near the entry point explaining the intake process and wait times.\n* Train multiple volunteers on the intake process to ensure a smooth operation even with unexpected arrivals.\n* Review and adjust the intake form as needed based on community feedback and repair volumes.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0102", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m helping organize a community art fair and need a simple way to assign booth numbers to vendors so setup day is less chaotic. Can you suggest an easy numbering system and a short instruction note we can send vendors?", "answer": "Here's a simple numbering system and a sample instruction note you can send to vendors:\n\n**Numbering System:**\n\nAssign a 2-3 digit number to each vendor, with the first digit indicating the row or section of the art fair where their booth will be located. For example:\n\n* Row 1: 01-09\n* Row 2: 10-19\n* Row 3: 20-29\n* Section A (special needs or ADA accessible): 30-39\n* Section B (food vendors): 40-49\n\nThis system will help vendors quickly identify where their booth is located and make setup easier to manage.\n\n**Instruction Note:**\n\n**Booth Assignment and Setup Instructions**\n\nDear Vendors,\n\nWe are excited to have you participating in our community art fair! To make setup day run smoothly, we have assigned a booth number to each vendor. Please find your assigned booth number below:\n\n[Insert vendor names and booth numbers]\n\n**Setup**\n\n* Arrive at the art fair between [insert setup times]\n* Identify your assigned booth number and set up according to the map provided\n* Please leave 3 feet of space between booths to allow for easy setup and foot traffic\n\n**Important:** Please note that you will be assigned a specific booth number, and we cannot accommodate changes to your booth location. If you have any vendor equipment or furniture that requires a particular setup or orientation, please let us know in advance so we can work with you to accommodate your needs.\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to reach out to us at [insert contact email or phone number]. We're looking forward to a wonderful community art fair!\n\nThank you for your participation!", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0116", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m hosting a neighborhood seed swap next month. Can you help me make a simple plan for how to set up the tables, label the seeds, and keep the exchange organized?", "answer": "A neighborhood seed swap sounds like a fun and community-building event. Here's a simple plan to help you set up and execute a successful seed swap:\n\n**Table Setup:**\n\n1. **Sorting tables:** Set up 2-3 tables with cloth or paper table covers to make the cleanup process easier. You'll need one table for seed categorization, one for seed display, and possibly a third for other items like seed starter kits, tools, or seed-saving supplies.\n2. **Seed categorization table:** Set up an alphabetized section for seeds by plant type (e.g., \"Beets, Carrots, Cucumbers\"). This will make it easier for attendees to find the specific seeds they're looking for.\n3. **Seed display table:** Organize seeds into categories (e.g., \"Vegetables, Fruits, Flowers\"). Use baskets, bins, or small containers to hold the seeds. Label each container clearly, and consider adding a small sign with a brief description of the seeds inside.\n4. **Check-in and welcome table:** Place a welcome sign at the entrance and have a table where people can check in, receive a ballot/ticket, and mark their seeds as they collect them.\n\n**Seed Labeling:**\n\n1. **Seal and label bags:** Use small paper bags or envelopes to store individual seed packets. Label each bag with the name of the seed, family member, and neighborhood swapper's name (if any).\n2. **Seed tags:** Create a system for labeling seeds in the display tables. Use cards or small pieces of paper with the following information:\n\t* Seed name\n\t* Family (e.g., Brassica, Solanaceae)\n\t* Seed saving notes (optional)\n\t* Any growing tips or cautions\n\n**Organizing the Exchange:**\n\n1. **Ballot system:** Create ballots or tickets with each attendee's name. Each time they collect a seed, they'll be given the corresponding ballot. This will help you keep track of exchanged seeds and prevent duplicates.\n2. **Scoring system:** Decide on a system to keep track of the number of seeds each participant has collected. This will help prevent people from \"hoarding\" seeds. Consider using stickers, stamps, or sticky notes to mark each collection.\n3. **Seed swaps:** Allow attendees to visit the seed display tables and collect a set number of seeds per purple/eligibility color (Choose from non advancing ballot). Once a seed has been picked, it can't be picked again.", "judge_response": "85", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 85}
+{"item_id": "effort_0113", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I need a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store. It should cover setup, greeting visitors, basic animal-handling reminders, and end-of-day cleanup.", "answer": "Here's a short orientation checklist for volunteers helping at a cat adoption event inside a pet store:\n\n**Pre-Event (Before the Store Opens)**\n\n1. **Setup**:\n\t* Arrive 30 minutes prior to store opening to help set up cat adoption stations, including:\n\t\t+ Cat carriers and toys\n\t\t+ Adoption information and application forms\n\t\t+ Food, water, and litter for the cats\n\t\t+ Comfortable areas for adopters to spend time with the cats\n2. **Familiarize yourself with the cats**:\n\t* Review the cat's bio and personality information to ensure you can provide accurate information to potential adopters\n\t* Ensure all cats are safe, happy, and comfortable in their carriers or designated areas\n\n**During the Event**\n\n1. **Greeting Visitors**:\n\t* Welcome visitors to the cat adoption event and provide information about the cats available for adoption\n\t* Encourage visitors to interact with the cats in a gentle and respectful manner\n2. **Basic Animal-Handling Reminders**:\n\t* Always wash your hands before and after handling cats\n\t* Handle each cat gently and briefly, allowing them to initiate contact\n\t* Monitor cat behavior and intervene if you notice signs of stress or agitation\n3. **Adoption Process**:\n\t* Assist potential adopters with the adoption process, including:\n\t\t+ Answering questions about the cats\n\t\t+ Reviewing adoption applications\n\t\t+ Helping with the adoption contract and payment process\n\n**Post-Event (At the End of the Day)**\n\n1. **Cleanup**:\n\t* Help clean up the cat adoption stations, including:\n\t\t+ Removing cat litter and soiled accessories\n\t\t+ Cleaning food and water bowls\n\t\t+ Restocking cat toys and supplies\n2. **Return cats to their carriers**:\n\t* Gently collect the cats and place them back in their carriers, ensuring they are safe and secure\n3. **Disassemble the adoption stations**:\n\t* Take down and store cat adoption materials, including information boards and application forms\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Be sensitive to cat signals and body language, and follow their lead in interactions\n* Keep the cat adoption area clean, quiet, and stress-free to ensure the cats' comfort and well-being\n* Encourage visitors to consider factors such as lifestyle, living situation, and family dynamics when choosing a cat to adopt\n\nRemember to stay alert, attentive, and compassionate throughout the event to ensure a positive experience for both the cats and the visitors. Good luck!", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0112", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I\u2019m putting together a simple orientation packet for a volunteer who will water and do quick visual checks on the plants in our small office during a three-week summer break. Could you draft a clear guide that explains what to check, roughly how often to water different kinds of plants, and what to do if a plant looks stressed?", "answer": "Here's a draft of a simple orientation packet for your volunteer:\n\n**Welcome to the Plant Care Volunteer Guide!**\n\nWe're excited to have you help keep our office plants happy and healthy while we're on summer break. This guide will walk you through what to check, when to water, and what to do if you notice any issues.\n\n**Scheduling**\n\nYou'll be visiting our office [insert frequency, e.g., daily, every other day] to water and perform quick visual checks on the plants. A schedule will be posted in the office for your reference.\n\n**What to Check**\n\n* Moisture levels: Stick your finger into the soil about an inch deep to check for moisture. If the soil feels dry, it's time to water.\n* Soil color: Healthy soil should be slightly moist and not too dry or waterlogged. If you notice any signs of waterlogging, such as mold or a sour smell, it's best to err on the side of caution and not water.\n* Leaf health: Check for any signs of stress, such as wilted or yellowing leaves. If you notice any issues, see the next section for guidance.\n* Pests: Keep an eye out for any signs of pests, such as spider mites, mealybugs, or scale.\n\n**Watering Schedule**\n\n* For most plants, water every [insert frequency, e.g., 2-3 days]. However, some plants may require more or less frequent watering. Here's a rough guide:\n\t+ Succulents and cacti: Water every 5-7 days\n\t+ Ferns and peace lilies: Water every 1-2 days\n\t+ Tropical plants (e.g., palms, bamboo): Water every 2-3 days\n* Water thoroughly: Watering plants requires giving them a good soaking. For planters, this means making sure the pot drains thoroughly, usually after 10-15 minutes.\n\n**What to Do if a Plant Looks Stressed**\n\n* **Wilting or yellowing leaves:** This can be due to over- or under-watering. Check the soil moisture and adjust your watering schedule accordingly.\n* **Mold or fungal growth:** This can be caused by overwatering or poor air circulation. Try reducing watering frequency and increasing air circulation around the plant.\n* **Pests:** Use insecticidal soap or neem oil to treat the plant. If the infestation is severe, notify us so we can provide further guidance.\n\n**Additional Tips**\n\n* Use room temperature water to avoid shocking the roots.\n* Avoid getting water on the plant's leaves or crown to prevent fungal growth.\n* Keep the water tray or drainage holes under the pot clear to prevent standing water.\n\n**Contact Us**\n\nIf you have any questions or concerns about the plants or this guide, don't hesitate to reach out to us. We're here to support you and the plants!\n\nThank you for your help in keeping our office plants healthy and thriving!\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0089", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I volunteer at a neighborhood tool library and need a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools before we put them into circulation. Can you make a practical checklist that non-experts could use?", "answer": "A tool library is a fantastic community resource. I'd be happy to help you create a simple inspection checklist for donated hand tools. Here's a practical and easy-to-follow checklist that non-experts can use:\n\n**General Inspection**\n\n1. **Overall condition**:\n\t* Is the tool clean and free of debris?\n\t* Are there any obvious signs of damage or wear?\n2. **Missing or loose parts**:\n\t* Are any handles, grips, or other parts missing?\n\t* Are any parts loose or detached?\n\n**Handle and Grip**\n\n1. **Handle integrity**:\n\t* Is the handle cracked, rotten, or otherwise damaged?\n\t* Are there any signs of wear or fatigue on the handle?\n2. **Grip condition**:\n\t* Is the grip worn, damaged, or loose?\n\n**Tool Head and Mechanism**\n\n1. **Tool head condition**:\n\t* Are there any signs of damage, wear, or corrosion on the tool head?\n\t* Is the tool head loose or free-wobbling?\n2. **Mekhanism (e.g. hinge, pivot, or moving parts)**\n\t* Are there any signs of wear, binding, or jamming?\n\t* Are all moving parts securely attached?\n\n**Safety Features**\n\n1. **Guard condition**:\n\t* Are any safety guards or shields cracked, broken, or missing?\n\t* Are guards loose or detached?\n2. **Cutting-edge condition**:\n\t* Are cutting edges worn, sharpened, or looking rough?\n\n**Other**\n\n1. **Labeling and information**:\n\t* Is the tool properly labeled with its name or purpose?\n\t* Are any special operating instructions or warning labels present?\n\n**Rating System**\n\nAssign a simple rating to each tool based on its condition:\n\n* **Green**: The tool is in excellent condition, with minimal signs of wear.\n* **Yellow**: The tool shows some wear or minor damage, but it's still usable.\n* **Red**: The tool is severely damaged, worn, or unsafe for use.\n\nCreate a log or database to track the inspection results and tool ratings. This will help you keep track of tools that need maintenance, replacement, or repair.\n\nRemember, this is a general checklist, and you can always add specific items depending on the types of tools your library receives and the level of expertise you want to require from volunteers.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
+{"item_id": "effort_0148", "paraphrase_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "question": "I need a short check-in checklist for volunteers helping at a bike helmet giveaway event for kids. Can you make a simple list they can follow so each child gets the right size and basic fit check?", "answer": "Here's a simple checklist for volunteers at a bike helmet giveaway event:\n\n**Helmet Fitting Checklist:**\n\n1. **Inspect the helmet:**\n\t* Make sure the helmet is not damaged or expired.\n\t* Check the manufacturer's guidelines for recommended head circumference.\n2. **Measure the child's head:**\n\t* Use a flexible tape measure or a string to measure the child's head circumference.\n\t* Take the measurement around the widest part of the head, just above the eyebrows and ears.\n3. **Select the correct size:**\n\t* Choose a helmet with a manufacturer's recommended size range that fits the child's head measurement.\n\t* Ensure the helmet is not too tight or too loose.\n4. **Check the fit:**\n\t* Gently place the helmet on the child's head, ensuring the front edge of the helmet sits at least 1 inch above the eyebrows.\n\t* The helmet should be level and not tilted forward or backward.\n\t* Check that the helmet is comfortable and doesn't obstruct the child's vision.\n5. **Double-check safety standards:**\n\t* Ensure the helmet meets current safety standards for bicycle helmets (e.g., CPSC, Snell, or equivalent).\n\t* Verify the helmet has certification stickers and meets the required safety standards.\n\nBy following this checklist, volunteers can ensure each child receives a proper-fitting, safe bike helmet.", "judge_response": "100", "bucket": "numeric", "score": 100}
